/* ---------------- ON TAP: beer, cider, sake and the wall ----------------
   Everything a bartender pours that was brewed rather than distilled, and the
   machine that pours it. This began as the beer domain inside Behind the
   Stick, which is where it had outgrown its home: it was the largest of that
   tab's six domains and the one it opened on, while the tab's own name
   promised the half of the job that is not a cocktail.

   Two arrays, deliberately the same shape as SERVICE_STUDY and SERVICE_REF,
   so renderOnTap is renderService with two identifiers swapped, the search
   index gets two more copies of lines it already has, and one check in
   tools/check.mjs closes both.

   THE REFERENCE CARDS ARE ALL FILED UNDER 'styles'. The other four sections
   draw rows alone, which the renderer's refs.length guard already covers.

   Two beer glasses stay behind in SERVICE_REF under glassware, and that is
   not an oversight: a lesson moves when the tab answering its question moves,
   and a reference card stays where the object lives. Somebody asking what a
   nonic is should find it beside the coupe and the Nick and Nora. Every style
   card here already carries its own Glass fact, so nothing is lost. */

const ONTAP_STUDY = [
  { key:"pour", title:"The Pour", rows:[
    ["The pour itself",
     "Rinse the glass in cold water, hold it at 45 degrees, and keep the rim about an inch below the faucet so the beer lands on the wall around the midpoint of the slope — not into the bottom, not from a height. At the halfway point straighten the glass and pour into the middle so the beer falls and builds head. Finish with one to one and a half fingers of foam, call it an inch. Never let the faucet touch the beer or the inside of the glass; that's a contamination path and it's the first thing an old bartender will notice about you."],
    ["Open the faucet all the way, every time",
     "The most common self-inflicted foam problem in the trade is a faucet opened halfway. A partly open faucet throttles the beer, drops its pressure across the restriction, and the CO2 comes out of solution as foam right there in the spout. Snap it fully open in one motion and fully closed in one motion. There is no such thing as a gentle draught pour, and an eased-shut faucet drips, and a dripping faucet grows bacteria."],
    ["Head is not theft",
     "A beer with no head still has all its CO2 in solution, which means it breaks out in the guest's stomach instead of in the glass, and it means the aroma — which lives in the foam — never reaches their nose. The foam also slows the beer's contact with air while they drink it. Know the honest arithmetic though. A shaker pint is 16 oz brim-full, and an inch across the top of one is roughly five ounces of space, so a modest head puts about 14 oz of liquid in front of the guest and a full inch of foam puts closer to 12. If your menu says 'pint,' pour into a 20 oz glass or a 16 oz-to-the-line glass, or stop calling it a pint."],
    ["Why the first pour off a fresh keg foams",
     "Three things at once: the beer was agitated by the truck and the walk-in floor, the line and faucet are full of warm beer or air, and the keg has to re-equalise after tapping. Let a delivered keg rest cold — two hours minimum, overnight is the real answer — then tap it, wait a minute, and pour the first pint into the dump. Nobody pays for that one. Rolling a keg to 'settle' it does the exact opposite."],
  ] },
  { key:"glass", title:"Beer-Clean Glass", rows:[
    ["Beer-clean glass",
     "Fat is foam's enemy. Cooking grease, lipstick, a fat-based sanitizer, leftover detergent, even a fingerprint inside the rim will collapse a head in seconds and leave the beer looking and tasting dead. This is why a beer 'pours flat' far more often than the gas is actually wrong. A dirty glass ruins a perfect keg, and the guest blames the beer."],
    ["The three tests",
     "SHEETING: wet the inside of the glass — clean glass sheds water in an even sheet, filmed glass breaks it into beads. SALT: sprinkle salt on the wet interior — it clings evenly all over a clean glass and refuses to stick where there's film, so the bare patches are your map. CLING: look at a full pint — on a beer-clean glass the bubbles rise from the base in a steady bead; bubbles stuck along the wall mean film. LACING is the after-the-fact test: a clean glass keeps rings of foam down the wall as the level drops, a dirty one comes back bare."],
    ["Washing and storing glassware",
     "Beer glasses want their own sink or washer, a non-fat detergent made for glassware, and a cold rinse after. Air-dry upright on a ribbed mat so air reaches the inside — never towel-dry, never stack them on a bar towel or a flat rubber mat, which traps stale air and gives you a glass that smells like the sink. And don't freeze pint glasses for craft beer: ice crystals over-nucleate the pour into foam and freezer odor goes straight into the beer. Cold rinse instead."],
  ] },
  { key:"system", title:"The System", rows:[
    ["Couplers",
     "D (American Sankey) covers the great majority of US domestic and craft kegs. S (European Sankey) handles most Belgian and German imports — Stella, Heineken, Amstel. U handles Guinness, Harp and Kilkenny. A and M are German slider types — Spaten, Paulaner, Hacker-Pschorr ride the A, and G turns up on a few British ales. Brand-to-coupler lists drift as importers change, so read the keg rather than trusting your memory of a chart. The probe geometry genuinely differs — if a coupler doesn't seat with a quarter turn and a firm push down, you have the wrong one. Forcing it damages the keg valve and sprays you with about eleven dollars of beer."],
    ["Keg sizes and what they yield",
     "Half barrel: 15.5 gal, 1,984 oz, 124 sixteen-ounce pours or 165 twelves, about 160 lb full. Sixth barrel (a 'sixtel'): 5.16 gal, 661 oz, about 41 pints, roughly 60 lb — the size most craft programs live on. Quarter barrel: 7.75 gal, about 62 pints, roughly 87 lb. European 50 L: 13.2 gal, about 105 pints, about 130 lb. Budget 3 to 5 percent loss to foam and line cleaning if the system is tight. The gap between 5 percent waste and 20 percent waste is a whole free keg a month on a busy line."],
    ["CO2 and applied pressure",
     "The gas does two jobs: it pushes the beer up the line, and it keeps the beer's own carbonation dissolved. Applied pressure has to overcome line resistance plus vertical rise, and it has to match what that beer wants at your cooler temperature — usually somewhere around 10 to 14 psi on a short direct-draw system at 38F. Too high and you slowly over-carbonate the keg and everything foams. Too LOW and the beer goes flat in the keg and foams in the line, because the CO2 breaks out of solution on the way up. Memorise that one: low pressure causes foam."],
    ["Nitro",
     "Nitro beers run on beer gas, roughly 75/25 nitrogen to CO2, pushed at 30 to 40 psi off its own regulator. Nitrogen barely dissolves in beer, so you can push it that hard without over-carbonating; the CO2 fraction just holds the beer's own fizz in place. Never push a nitro keg with straight CO2 at those pressures — you'll turn a stout into a foam bomb that no amount of resting will fix. The faucet carries a restrictor plate, a disc of tiny holes, that shears the gas into microbubbles, and that's the cascade. Glass at 45 degrees, handle pulled all the way toward you for the restricted pour, fill about three-quarters, set it down and let it settle for roughly ninety seconds, then top it upright by pushing the handle away for the slow creamer pour. It takes about two minutes and there is no shortcut. It wants to be warmer than your lagers, around 42F."],
    ["38 degrees",
     "Cooler at 38F, and every foot of beer line at 38F too. Beer holds CO2 in solution as a function of temperature and pressure; warm it and the gas comes out, and it comes out inside your line where you can't see it. Above about 40F you foam on every pour. Set the box below about 34F and the coldest corner near the evaporator can drop under 28F, which is where beer actually freezes, and frozen beer never fully recovers its carbonation. When a style card says 50 to 55F, that's what the beer wants in the glass, not a second cooler setting — a pour picks up two or three degrees on the walk out and a dark beer opens up after a minute in the hand. The faucet is the warmest point in the whole system, which is exactly why the first glass after a twenty-minute lull foams and the second one is honest. One box, one temperature: 38F is a storage-and-carbonation number set by the CO2 equilibrium, not a service preference. The style temperatures in the reference entries are what the beer should be when it reaches the palate, and it gets there by warming in the glass and by your choice of glassware — never by running a second, warmer tap."],
    ["Line cleaning cadence",
     "Caustic every two weeks, recirculated, with faucets disassembled and soaked at the same time. Acid every three months to strip beerstone, which is calcium oxalate and which caustic will not touch. Couplers and FOB detectors go on the same schedule. Never put bleach in a beer line. The why: beer lines grow biofilm in days, and biofilm gives you diacetyl (butter, butterscotch), acetic sourness, or a wet-cardboard note that no amount of blaming the brewery will fix. A bar with clean lines pours a noticeably better version of the exact same beer, and it costs nothing but discipline."],
    ["FIFO on kegs",
     "Tape the delivery date on every keg the moment it hits the cooler floor, and stack so the oldest comes out first. Rough cold shelf life: macro lager 90 to 120 days, craft 45 to 60, West Coast IPA 45, hazy IPA 30 to 45. Worth knowing: most keg beer is unpasteurized even when the same brand's bottles aren't, which is why the cold chain matters more on draught than anywhere else in your building. Hazies die first and loudly, turning from citrus to onion and cardboard. Write the tap date on the keg too and note the day it blows — two months of that tells you your real depletion rate per line, which is the entire basis of ordering correctly. A keg that warmed and re-chilled has aged more than the calendar says."],
    ["When a keg blows mid-rush",
     "Tell the guest first. 'That one just blew, give me two minutes.' Then close the faucet, pull the coupler handle up to cut the gas, twist the coupler off, wipe the new keg's neck (it rode on a truck floor and it's a food-contact surface), seat the coupler with a quarter turn, push the handle down, wait thirty to sixty seconds, and pour the first pint into the dump. Never hand over the foam pour you knew was coming — that's a comp and a bad review in one move. If it was the last one, 86 it in the POS and tell the floor in the same breath, or servers will keep selling a beer you do not have."],
    ["Kegs are the heaviest thing you touch",
     "A full half barrel is about 160 lb of steel and liquid with no good handhold. Lift with your legs, tip and walk it on its chime rather than carrying it, never take one down stairs alone, and don't do any of it in canvas shoes. The injuries kegs cause aren't the frequent ones — that's cuts and slips — they're the ones that end careers, crushed feet and wrecked backs. Nothing about a Saturday rush is worth either."],
    ["Faucet anatomy and the nightly cap",
     "Other rows own the snap and the cleaning calendar; this is what's actually in your hand. From the shank forward it's the bonnet, the collar that screws the faucet on, the seat washer it tightens against, the body with its bore and spout, the shaft that slides inside, the lever that drives the shaft, and the friction ring that holds lever tension. The distinction that matters is where the shaft seals. A rear-sealing faucet, the classic chrome one, seals at the back against that seat washer, so the bore in front of the seal sits wet with air on it every time you close, and by morning that's a stale film of yeast and sugar you pour through. A forward-sealing faucet seals at the nozzle instead, so the body stays full of beer with no air in it, and that is why it stays sweet between cleanings, not because it runs dry. Buy stainless anyway: chrome-plated brass wears through and pits, and pitted plating holds bacteria and puts a metallic edge on a pale beer. Then the habit nobody keeps. Every night, rinse the nozzle and cap or plug each faucet. It keeps fruit flies, dust and airborne mold off a wet spout while you're closed, and costs you a few seconds a tap."],
    ["What a beer line actually is",
     "A beer line is not tubing, it is calibrated resistance. Cheap clear vinyl at 3/16 inch inner diameter is charted at about 3 psi per foot, and the line's whole job is to spend the pressure the gas applies, so the beer reaches the faucet at nearly nothing while keeping its carbonation. So you size it by arithmetic, not habit: take your applied pressure, subtract about a psi for the faucet and half a psi for every foot the faucet sits above the keg, and divide by 3. At a 12 psi house number that is three or four feet, which is why direct draw boxes ship with five, and why plenty of real systems want more still, since the charts overstate what soft vinyl actually delivers. Vinyl also takes up hop oil and sour character, clouds with age, and wants replacing yearly; barrier tubing is multi-layer, absorbs nothing, cleans properly, and lasts years. Now the mistake. A tap foams, so someone trims a foot off to speed it up. That discards 3 psi of the resistance you sized, and beer that reaches the faucet still under pressure and moving fast breaks out as foam on its way through. An unbalanced line is cured by adding line. Cutting never cures it."],
  ] },
  { key:"gas", title:"Gas & Balance", rows:[
    ["Balancing the line",
     "Balancing means sizing the run so its restriction eats nearly all the pressure you apply, leaving about a pound at the faucet: enough to move beer, not enough to blast it. The published resistance for vinyl, per foot: 3/16 inch inner diameter about 3 psi, 1/4 inch about 0.85, 5/16 inch about 0.4, 3/8 inch about 0.2. The lower figures you see quoted, 2.2 and 0.5, belong to polyethylene and barrier tubing, so know which line you are holding. Add roughly 0.5 psi for every foot the beer climbs. Work it like this: 12 psi applied, a 4 foot rise eats 2, leave 1 at the faucet, and the remaining 9 divided by 3 is three feet of 3/16 vinyl. Cut five and trim, because published resistance runs optimistic and you can shorten a line but never un-cut one. What you are aiming at is near 2 oz a second, a 16 oz glass in about 8 seconds, a touch quicker once the head takes its share. Time one. If it lands in 4, the run is under-restricted, and the fix is length, not a handle held halfway. Line is cheap and it is the only part of this you can actually adjust."],
    ["Long draw and glycol",
     "A long draw is not direct draw with more hose. Once the run passes about 25 feet, and it can be hundreds, the beer travels in a trunk bundle: several beer lines strapped against a glycol supply and return, foamed and jacketed as one cable. A power pack holds the glycol at 28 to 30F and pumps it out and back, so the glycol chills the line along its whole length and the beer still reaches the faucet near 38F. Cooler air does nothing past the cooler wall, which tells you where the warm spots hide. The glycol has to run up inside the tower and end within a few inches of the shank, or the top of the tower and the shank sit at room temperature and your first pour of the hour is foam. Same for any length spliced through a wall without the return coming back, and for any keg or jumper staged outside the box. Put a FOB on every long line: it holds the beer in the line when the keg blows, so you swap, reset it, and pour instead of purging 60 feet of 3/8 inch, which is about 0.73 oz a foot, or 44 oz down the drain."],
    ["Blended gas",
     "Straight CO2 can't run a long draw, and the reason is arithmetic you can't argue with. A 60 foot run plus rise needs 25 to 35 psi applied to move beer at a working speed. At 38F, 30 psi of pure CO2 drives the keg toward 4.3 volumes, and it was packaged near 2.4 to 2.7. The keg doesn't refuse. It takes the gas on over a week or two, and the tap that poured clean on Tuesday foams all weekend while you blame the cooler. The fix is a blend, and the rule is that only the CO2 fraction counts against the beer: nitrogen barely dissolves, so it adds pressure without adding condition. Match the fraction to the pressure. At 30 psi, 60/40 CO2 to nitrogen holds about 2.6 volumes; run 70/30 at that same 30 psi and you're back to roughly 3.0 and foaming, so 70/30 belongs nearer 25 psi. Mind the order of those numbers, because beer gas is 75/25 the other way, and on an ale it drives toward one volume and flat. Buy premix cylinders for a handful of lines. Once you're buying premix by the pallet, price a blender panel fed by bulk gas, which re-dials as the beer or the cooler changes."],
    ["One number per beer",
     "The CO2 row owns the house number, the figure that works when every keg on the wall sits near 2.5 volumes. This is the case where one number is wrong. Carbonation is set at the brewery and it isn't a preference: a Bavarian hefeweizen goes out at 3.3 to 4.5 volumes, an English ale or porter at 1.8 to 2.2. At 38F the hefe needs roughly 20 to 32 psi and the ale roughly 4 to 8. Put both on 12 and the hefe foams from the first pint, because the excess gas breaks out where pressure drops, and within a week it has given that carbonation up to the headspace, while the ale has quietly gained some. So set the primary on the tank to your highest requirement plus a couple of psi, because a secondary can only regulate down, never up; then a secondary per keg, or per bank of like beers, puts each where the brewery put it. Nitro runs its own gas off its own regulator, so keep it off this manifold. Remember the other half. A hefe line at 28 psi needs more restriction than an ale line at 6. Different pressure means different line length. One without the other just moves the problem to a different tap."],
    ["Volumes of CO2 and the walk-in chart",
     "Every beer arrives from the brewery already carbonated to a number, and the gas holds that number while it pushes; it does not create it. A volume is one beer's worth of CO2 gas, at atmospheric pressure, dissolved in the beer, and styles sit far apart: British ales 1.5 to 2.0, most ales 2.2 to 2.6, American lager 2.5 to 2.8, saison 2.5 to 3.5, hefeweizen 3.6 to 4.5. True cask sits lower still and runs on no gas. The chart taped inside the walk-in crosses temperature against pressure and hands you volumes, and your house number at box temperature lands around 2.4 to 2.7. That is why lagers and pale ales behave on one setting and the wheat does not. A hefeweizen at 3.8 volumes wants about 25 psi, and five feet of 3/16 inch line resists only about 15 psi, so the rest reaches the faucet as foam. The fix is resistance plus its own gas: 3/16 vinyl eats roughly 3 psi per foot, so an eight to ten foot run carries 25 psi and still pours a pint. Your taps share one manifold, though, so turning the house regulator up over-carbonates every other keg. The wheat wants a secondary regulator of its own, or it keeps dying by Thursday."],
    ["CO2 will kill you and it is silent",
     "You won't smell it or see it. CO2 is half again as heavy as air, so a leak pools low instead of drifting off, and the cooler fan spreads it through the box, so standing upright saves nobody. Half a percent over a shift is the workplace limit, 4 percent is called immediately dangerous, and 7 to 10 percent puts a person down in minutes. A 20 lb cylinder is about 5,000 litres of gas, enough to carry a 4,000 cubic foot cellar past 4 percent. (1) Cylinder strapped upright, never loose. (2) Valve cap on before you move it; a snapped valve makes the bottle a projectile. (3) Closing the regulator is not closing the gas, so shut the cylinder valve at close. (4) Soapy water on every fitting after any change. (5) The tank gauge sits on liquid CO2 and barely moves until the liquid is gone, so it cannot show an overnight leak; with the valve shut, note the applied-pressure gauge at close and read it at open, and a drop with nothing poured is a leak. (6) A monitor and alarm in the cellar, not a nose. And if someone goes down and doesn't come back, don't follow, because more than half of confined space deaths are the rescuers."],
  ] },
  { key:"beyond", title:"Cask, Pumps & Growlers", rows:[
    ["Cask ale and the beer engine",
     "This is the one beer that lives outside your 38F box. Cask ale is still working: live yeast and a little sugar in the cask, its own CO2, no gas behind it, pulled by a beer engine that moves about half a pint a stroke, so a pint is two pulls. It carries 1.0 to 1.5 volumes, half what a keg lager holds. A cask pint is not a flat pint; sent back as flat, it is carbonated, just quietly. Two bungs matter: the keystone in the head, where the tap is driven, and the shive on top, where the spile goes. Let it settle, then vent 24 to 48 hours ahead with a soft spile, a porous peg that lets the excess blow off; when it stops hissing, swap in a hard spile. Pull that spile while you serve, or the pump fights a vacuum, and seat it at close. Serve at 52 to 55F, cellar cool or a cask jacket, never the cold box. A sparkler knocks CO2 out into a tight creamy head and carries hop aroma off with it, northern practice and southern heresy, so follow the brewery. Once broached you have about three days; past that, cardboard and vinegar are a real fault."],
    ["A party pump is an afternoon tool",
     "A party pump pushes room air, which is 21 percent oxygen and essentially no CO2, and that second half is the part people miss. Carbonation stays in solution against CO2 pressure specifically, so with air above the beer the dissolved gas walks out no matter how hard you pump, and the beer flattens while it stales. The oxygen gives you wet cardboard, sherry and a papery finish inside 12 to 24 hours, faster in a warm room, and that part is one way. You cannot pull oxygen back out, so a keg you pumped tonight is not a keg you put back on gas tomorrow and sell. A pump is for a backyard afternoon and nothing else. When the gas runs dry mid service the first move is your spare CO2 tank, which is why you keep one full, strapped and checked on every open. No spare means 86 the tap and sell cans. It never means reach for a pump."],
    ["Jockey box and outdoor service",
     "The cellar rows here assume a walk-in and a short line; a jockey box is neither, and the coil is the whole story. Published resistance charts put 3/8 inch stainless coil near 0.2 psi per foot, so a 50 foot coil is about 10 psi of drop before you count the rise or the faucet. That is why a jockey box wants 20 to 30 psi applied when your bar runs 10 to 14, and why turning the gas down at a hot festival because it's foaming does the same thing it does indoors: it makes it worse. Pack ice and water, not ice alone, because a dry pile of cubes leaves air gaps against the coil and a gap of air is insulation. Give the packed box fifteen or twenty minutes before any beer goes through; the ice chills the coil, and the pint or two you dump only clears warm beer and air. Then know the limit. A keg at 75F needs around 30 psi just to hold the carbonation it already has, before the coil takes its ten, so you cool the keg rather than chase it with gas. Bury it in the tub, and put the regulator back to your house number before the keg returns to the walk-in."],
    ["Growlers and crowlers",
     "Bottle and can service to a seated guest is another row's business; this is the fill you hand out the door, and the enemy is air. A growler is 64 oz; a crowler is a 32 oz can seamed on the spot, with no thread to leak past and no light. (1) Purge the vessel with CO2, which is roughly 1.5 times as dense as air and does displace it, but it mixes back in within a minute, so fill right away. (2) Fill from the bottom through a tube or filler spout so the beer doesn't splash, foam and give up its carbonation on the way in. (3) Cap glass while it's still foaming; the point isn't zero volume, it's that foam leaves CO2 in the headspace instead of air. Crowlers are the exception: fill to the seamer's line or they won't seam. Counter-pressure, where the bar has one, gets closest to what the tap poured. Honest life: a plain growler fill is two or three days, a purged one a week, a crowler two to four weeks cold, anything opened is that day. And a guest's own growler out of a hot trunk isn't a container, it's a culture. If you can't see it clean inside, don't fill it."],
  ] },
  { key:"faults", title:"Tasting & Faults", rows:[
    ["Foamy pour, in order of likelihood",
     "In order, ninety seconds total. (1) Did you open the faucet all the way in one snap? (2) Pour a full glass and dump it — if the second pours clean, it was a warm idle faucet and you're done. (3) Look at the walk-in thermometer and the door. Every tap foaming at once is almost always a propped door or a dying cooler. (4) Look at the CO2 — tank gauge for gas remaining, regulator for applied pressure against your house number. Empty tank and drifted regulator both foam. (5) Lift the keg. Light means it's blowing, and the last few pints always foam. (6) Walk the line for a kink, a warm run outside the cold, or a loose faucet. If none of that fixes it, 86 the tap, sell the can, and leave a written note for whoever cleans lines."],
    ["Flat pour, in order of likelihood",
     "(1) Pour a fresh one into a glass from a different rack. If that one heads up and laces, your glassware is the fault and you have a whole rack to rewash. (2) Check the CO2 tank and the applied pressure — a slow leak or a drifted regulator flattens a keg over a couple of days, and the beer will taste correct but go dead in the glass. (3) Check the keg's pack date and how long it's been tapped; five weeks on a slow line and the beer is just tired. (4) If it's one guest's glass and the beer has sat twenty minutes, that isn't a fault, that's physics — offer a fresh one anyway and don't lecture them about it."],
    ["Taste every tap in twenty seconds",
     "Tasting on shift isn't a ceremony and it isn't only for complaints: it's two ounces and twenty seconds. (1) Hold it to the light for three seconds and read color, clarity and whether the head holds. (2) Nose it while the foam is still standing, two or three short sniffs rather than one hard pull, because your nose adapts within seconds. Bursting bubbles throw volatiles out of the liquid, so a breaking head is the loudest that beer smells: nose it first and the butterscotch off a dirty line or the green apple off a young keg reaches you instead of the room. (3) Take a mouthful, not a sip, and move it around for three seconds. Every part of the tongue reads every taste, so that isn't hunting a bitter zone at the back; bitterness is simply slow to arrive and slow to leave. (4) Spit, then exhale through your nose. The retronasal pass is where the finish lives and where oxidation reads as wet cardboard, lifted by your mouth's warmth. Twenty taps swallowed is not a shift you can work. (5) Count the finish, then write it down. Every tap, every Monday, two lines each. Memory says the beer is fine. A notebook says tap 6 has been drifting for three weeks."],
    ["What appearance actually tells you",
     "Cloudy doesn't mean spoiled, so stop dumping beer over haze. Clarity has three causes and one is a fault. Chill haze is protein hydrogen-bonding to polyphenol in the cold, worst near 32F and back in solution by about 68F, so it clears as the glass warms. Deliberate haze never clears: yeast in a hefeweizen or anything bottle-conditioned, unmalted wheat in a wit, and in a hazy IPA not yeast but hop polyphenol bound to wheat and oat protein, which is why a centrifuged hazy pours murky. The fault is the beer that poured bright last month, pours dull now, stays dull as it warms, and shows fine strands when you tilt it. That is rope, a sugar thrown by Pediococcus or Lactobacillus, so it is biological, and your line is likelier than the keg. Head retention sets protein and hop acids against fat and alcohol, and ethanol thins foam at every strength, not past a threshold, so don't chase an inch on a barrel-aged stout. A slow bead reads nucleation sites, not carbonation. Color is SRM, 2 to 4 for a pale lager and 30 to 40 for an imperial stout, roughly double in EBC, and it predicts nothing about strength: a dry stout is 4 percent, a tripel 9."],
    ["Bitterness is a number and a feeling",
     "IBU is International Bitterness Units, and it's a lab number: acidify the beer, extract into solvent, read absorbance at 275 nm, one unit being roughly 1 mg per litre of isomerised alpha acid. The assay catches other hop material too, so it's close to that, not identical. It measures what's dissolved, not what the guest feels, and past about 100 the palate stops telling the difference, near the ceiling of what stays in solution. That gap is why a 70 IBU hazy tastes softer than a 35 IBU pilsner: the hazy carries oats and wheat protein, finishes sweeter near 1.016 against 1.008, runs chloride-forward water that rounds bitterness, and takes much of its hops cold in the dry hop, which adds aroma and measured units without isomerising, while a hot whirlpool does. The pilsner is bone dry, and where it's brewed sulfate-forward that sharpens the same bitterness into an edge. The printed figure is a packaging-day measurement that never changes, while six weeks in the keg rounds off the beer. Print it and you've asked guests to compare two numbers that mean different things, and whoever ordered by the big number gets a beer that tastes weak. Describe the finish instead: this one finishes dry and snappy, that one's soft and the bitterness barely lands."],
    ["You cannot taste what nobody showed you",
     "Nobody is born knowing what diacetyl tastes like, and a confident palate is not a calibrated one. The published threshold is near 0.1 mg/L, a tenth of a part per million; sensitive tasters call it around 0.02 and others need several times the book figure before butter reads at all. So 'tastes fine to me' is evidence about you before it is evidence about the beer. Know the source, too: diacetyl comes either from the brewery, where yeast left alpha-acetolactate behind and it converted outside the cell, or from Pediococcus living in your line and making it directly. Train the cheap way. Taste it spiked into clean beer beside an unspiked glass, warm, because cold hides it and the pint that sat ten minutes shows it, and sniff in short pulls, because your nose adapts to butter in seconds. A fault kit covering the dozen faults you actually meet runs a couple hundred dollars, about what one craft sixtel costs. Then one bartender is a flag and a second fresh nose on a fresh pour is a verdict that the beer is off; which keg or which line comes after. And a low butter note is to style in Czech pale lager and some English ales, so taste before pulling the handle."],
    ["Is it a fault, or is it the style",
     "Much of what comes back over the bar is the beer doing its job. Ask three things before you call a fault: does the style expect this flavor, at this intensity, and is it on every beer off that tap. A Czech pale lager is allowed a whisper of diacetyl, butter at or just under the 0.1 mg/L threshold, a trace the tradition accepts. A pale lager can show cooked-corn DMS, threshold near 30 to 50 ppb, boiled off pale malt but never all of it, so a trace is style and a bowl of creamed corn is not. Green-apple acetaldehyde, threshold 10 to 20 mg/L, is the rung below ethanol yeast normally takes back: a trace rides in a big pale lager, a lungful is unfinished beer or a dirty line. A gose is sour on purpose, an old ale sherry and leather, a rauchbier campfire from beechwood-kilned malt. Guests misread the hefeweizen banana and the gose salt most, so pull the style card rather than talk over them. Read bandaid carefully. In a gueuze or a Flanders red it is Brett's 4-ethylphenol and the beer is correct. Off a clean lager line, bandaid with plastic is chlorophenol, chlorine meeting the beer's own phenols at a few parts per billion. Pull that tap."],
    ["When the guest is right",
     "Sometimes the beer really is bad, and the job is making that cost the guest nothing, dignity included. Take the glass and say 'let me get you another one,' full stop, before you decide anything about who is right. Then prove it, not from their glass: a greasy glass throws this exact complaint, so their pint cannot tell you beer from rack. Pour a fresh sample off that tap into a clean taster and read it out of their sightline, because sniffing a pint in front of a guest reads as an accusation either way. Clean sample means the glass or their palate, so bring the replacement and say nothing about thresholds, lines or the brewery. Faulted sample and you pull that handle on the first complaint, not the second; a second guest is just one more who drank it. Log it either way: tap, time, beer, what they said, what you tasted. A craft sixtel is about forty-one pints, so a 110 dollar keg puts under three dollars of beer into that comp, the cheapest information you will buy all shift. Every tap showing it is the cooler or the lines; one tap alone is that line or that keg, and do not dump that keg, because the claim lives in it."],
  ] },
  { key:"cellar", title:"Cellar & Condition", rows:[
    ["Reading a keg collar",
     "Couplers and rotation are settled elsewhere; read the paper and the steel. Two dates turn up on a collar and they aren't the same. The pack date is the day the keg was filled, the only honest number on there. A best-by is the brewery's guess, commonly pack plus 90 to 180 days, and it outlasts the window the beer pours well in, so it isn't permission to serve. Work back from one only if you know that window; otherwise call the distributor. A four-digit Julian stamp mixes a year digit with the day of the year in either order, so the three digits between 001 and 366 are the day, the loose digit the year: 6145 is day 145 of 2026. If both readings scan, ask. The chime carries what paper can't lose, capacity in gallons and liters and a tare weight, so a scale tells you what's left. The ownership legend is the deposit chain, commonly $30 to $50 a shell, well under replacement cost and refunded only where the keg came from; scrapping one is theft several states wrote scrapyard laws to stop. The adjectives are marketing. Brand, style, brewer and the government warning are what the law requires; ABV often isn't, so ask the brewery when a count depends on it."],
    ["Tap handle honesty",
     "Five things in your building claim to know what's on tap: the handle, the POS button, the printed or chalked list, your online menu, and down in the cooler, the keg that line is coupled to. Only the last is true. The rest are claims, and the handle is the one the guest believes. Here's the tempting wrong move. A keg blows at eight, you swap in whatever's cold, and the old handle stays up because you'll fix the board at close. Between eight and close you sell forty pints of a beer nobody ordered. Serving one brand while representing another violates most state liquor codes, and the handle is the representation; what follows varies by state, from a notice to a fine. It's also the first thing a brand rep or inspector checks. So change it at swap time, in one trip: handle down, new handle up, POS button renamed, board corrected, line tagged, floor told, online menu flagged. No handle for the new keg? A blank handle with a written tag beats the wrong brand. Say the new ABV out loud, because the count you're keeping on a guest just moved. And if the old handle said cider or gluten-free, that isn't a branding problem, it's somebody's health."],
    ["Receiving a delivery",
     "The driver's clock is not your clock, and the second you sign that invoice the problems on it become yours. So sign last. (1) Count against the invoice line by line, brand and size, because two sixtels are the same silver cylinder on a hand truck and the miscount surfaces at eleven Friday. (2) Hand every neck and valve, judging sticky not wet: a cold keg sweats on a warm dock, so water tells you nothing; tacky or beery means that valve has wept. It lost carbonation outward; a sealed keg holds pressure and cannot pull air in. (3) Temperature is a cold-chain question, not a touch test: steel at 65F still feels cold to your hand. If your wholesaler ships refrigerated, use the thermometer you check the walk-in with. Warm is a shelf-life call, not a health-code one. (4) A bent chime stops a keg sitting flat; a dent near the valve can distort the spear so no coupler seats. (5) Read each date code off the collar or cap, note whether it is a pack date or a best-by, and copy it onto the invoice as well as the tape. Too old is a refusal, and only at the door. Write refusals on both copies and get the driver's signature on yours."],
    ["The cold chain, warm then cold",
     "Cold isn't a preference, it's a clock, and the staling rate roughly doubles for every 18F above your cooler. That's the Q10 rule, an approximation and a kind one, since published values for beer run 2 to 3 per 18F, so doubling is the gentle end. A keg at 56F ages about twice as fast as the same keg at 38F. At 74F it's about four times. Run that on a real week: seven days forgotten on a warm dock at 74F burns about 28 days of cold shelf life, so a keg only a week off the invoice drinks three weeks older than the calendar shows. Eight hours in a 78F back hallway while you finish prep costs about a day and a half. The wrong belief is that putting it back in the box undoes it. It doesn't. Oxidation and the staling reactions behind cardboard and sherry run one direction only; cold slows the clock, it never winds it back, and hop aroma that fell out in the warm doesn't return at 38F. So read every cold shelf life you work from as a best case, meet the load at the truck rather than at the end of prep, and make twenty minutes to the cooler door your house rule."],
    ["Skunked is light, not age",
     "The fault has a mechanism and it isn't age. Riboflavin in the beer absorbs light in roughly the 350 to 500 nm band, UV plus the blue end of visible, and what it kicks off tears apart the hop's iso-alpha acids. One fragment recombines with sulfur into 3-methyl-2-butene-1-thiol, and your nose reads that at a few parts per trillion, which is why a trace ruins a whole glass. In direct sun a clear bottle goes in seconds and green in minutes, though a few clear-bottle brands are bittered with light-stable hop extract and never skunk at all. Brown blocks most of that band and buys days, and back-bar fluorescent tubes get there over days to weeks. Cans and kegs protect the beer in the package, but nothing protects the pour: a canned or draught pint left on a sunlit patio table skunks in under a minute, so that complaint can be real. If the can sat in shade, it isn't skunk, it's oxidation, wet cardboard and sherry, which is a date and storage problem, not a light one. Move clear and green bottles out of the window and out from under the display light, keep pours off sunlit tables, and stop using 'skunked' as a word for 'old.'"],
    ["Packaging formats and what each protects",
     "Each format defends against a different enemy, which tells you what to reject on the dock. A can blocks light completely and, off a well-run line, picks up less oxygen at fill than a bottling line. Its weak point is the double seam at the chime: a dent there is a rejected case, because a flexed seam leaks slowly, losing CO2 and taking on air. A smooth sidewall dent is cosmetic and you can sell it; a sharp crease can fracture the lining inside and cannot. Bottles trade away the light protection and seal under a crown whose liner breathes a little. Cork and cage is a pressure closure, not a promise of live yeast: plenty of corked beer is filtered, and most bottle-conditioned beer wears a plain crown. Sediment is the signal, and those stand upright so the yeast packs down and pours clear. Then pasteurization, where macro packaging gets its long dates. Tunnel pasteurization runs the sealed package through hot water, measured in pasteurization units, typically 15 to 30 PU, one PU being a minute at 60C, 140F. Flash pasteurization heats the beer to about 71 to 74C, 160 to 165F, for 15 to 30 seconds before filling, and is gentler on aroma. Draught is usually neither, which the keg rotation entry covers."],
    ["Oxygen is the clock",
     "Oxygen is the last ingredient in the can, and nobody meant to add it. Total package oxygen, headspace plus dissolved, is what sets the shelf life before the beer ever reaches you: a well-run modern line lands under about 50 parts per billion and the best run under 30, while a struggling one runs into the hundreds. How fast that clock ticks is temperature, and that part is yours. Staling chemistry roughly doubles in rate for every 10C, about 18F, of warmth, so a case parked in a 75F stockroom is aging something like four times faster than the same case in a 38F cooler, whatever the printed date says. What you taste is not sour and not obviously spoiled, and that's the trap. Hop aroma goes first. Nothing escapes a sealed can; the aroma compounds react, oxygen and acid rearranging them into duller molecules, so citrus and pine flatten to something vaguely herbal, then to nothing. Bitterness softens next. Meanwhile oxidation builds its own sweetness, so the beer reads rounder, maltier, a little honeyed, and finally papery or like wet cardboard. By three months warm, it is unmistakable. Nobody sends that back. They just never order it again."],
    ["Which beers improve and which only decline",
     "Beer doesn't age like wine, and 'cellar' is not a synonym for 'didn't sell.' Two shapes keep, for different reasons. Strong and malty is one: roughly 8% and up, big body, dark or heavy grain, no dependence on hop aroma. Imperial stout, barleywine, Belgian quad and strong dark, and the barrel-aged versions. There the oxidation that wrecks a pale beer reads as sherry, dried fruit and toffee, with enough malt underneath to carry it. Two to five years from the pack date. Live culture is the other, which breaks that rule: lambic and gueuze are pale and about 5 to 6%, but live Brett and acidity keep them working in the bottle for a decade or two. A kettle sour or fruited gose has neither; drink those fresh. Store bottles dark and upright at 50 to 55F in a dry room, never by warming the walk-in, which stays at 38. Everything pale and delicate dies in place: IPA, pilsner, hefeweizen, cream ale. Nothing escapes a sealed can, the aroma breaks down where it sits, and the hazy IPA style card gives you the number. The fast list gets ordered to sell out in weeks and discounted at week three, not hoped over. The cellar list gets bought on purpose, in quantity, once a year."],
    ["Cellar temperature is not one number",
     "One box, one temperature still owns the draught system, and nothing here warms a beer line; it just stops at the cold box door. A bottle program runs two storage temperatures, not one. Anything you sell cold and fast, keg or package, holds at 38F. Bottles you mean to keep, sours, strong Belgians, barrel-aged stouts, want a dry cellar or dedicated fridge at 50 to 55F: cool enough to hold staling back, warm enough that they still develop. Anything hoppy or pale stays cold and never enters that room. Cask is the one exception on dispense, around 54F, published standards landing between 52 and 55F, because a cask is a live, vented, near-atmospheric vessel still finishing, not a keg you warmed up. Steady beats exact: staling roughly doubles for every 18F, so a room held at 57F beats one swinging 48F to 68F, where the hot half costs more than the cold half gives back. Store everything upright. A crown needs no wetting, and a standing bottle-conditioned beer packs its yeast into a disc instead of a stripe down the wall you will pour. A beer cork is held compressed by the neck and caged against the pressure, sealed by a humid headspace, so laying it down only soaks beer into cork."],
    ["86ing a beer without losing the sale",
     "This is the 86 you can see coming: a sixtel down to its last few pints on a Thursday with no replacement until Tuesday. That is a countdown, not an 86 yet, and the order matters. Tell the floor first, with a number: four left, sell them now, stop recommending it. Make somebody say the number back. Kill the button early and you throw away money you already own, though the tail of a keg foams, so dump what isn't a beer. The moment it is truly gone, 86 it in the POS, because a ticket for a beer you don't have costs a void, a walk back to the table and an apology. Then the handle, and with it the tap list, the board, whatever the website shows, because the list is what most guests read and a live handle is how the next shift pours air. Have the replacement in your mouth before anyone asks, and check the walk-in for the same beer in cans first. Not 'that's gone,' but 'that one finished tonight, the pale on six is the closest thing we have and I'd put you there.' A named substitute keeps the sale. A shrug turns it into a water."],
    ["Depletion, par, and the dead line",
     "Two months of tap dates and blow dates collapse into one number per line: kegs per week. A line that empties a sixtel's 41 pints in seven days is pouring about six a day, and nothing in the glass is more than a week past tap. A line that takes five weeks on the same sixtel is pouring beer 35 days past tap at the end, on top of the weeks it aged between packaging and your coupler. Measure that against the style's window, not your patience. A hazy IPA gets 30 to 45 days total, so the bottom third of that keg was gone before you sold it. Set par off the measured rate rather than the order sheet: weekly usage times the number of weeks between deliveries, rounded up to whole kegs, plus one keg of cushion on anything moving faster than a keg a week. Then cull honestly. A tap that can't empty a keg inside that beer's own shelf life isn't a cult favorite, it's a scheduled complaint. It holds a cooler slot, a coupler and a line you clean every two weeks, and it hands a tired pint to the one guest who trusted you enough to order the odd thing. Twelve taps you turn beat twenty you don't."],
    ["Beer you cannot sell",
     "A tired or faulted keg has three honest destinations and the guest is not one. First prove it is the keg and not your lines: biofilm throws these same notes, so if every tap shows it, or a fresh keg pours clean on that line, you have nothing to claim. (1) The claim, which dies the moment beer leaves the keg. Most distributors carry an out of code allowance and want the same evidence: the product still in the keg, the date code photographed, the call made while a rep can see it. Expect credit or replacement. A dumped keg is an unprovable claim that lands in your cost of goods. (2) The taste glass, once the rep clears it. Pour one and pass it down to smell: butterscotch is diacetyl, wet cardboard is oxidation, vinegar or a ropy slickness means infection. Spit if you taste, and know your state's rule on drinking on shift. Plenty of people cannot smell diacetyl at all, so never rest a call on one nose. (3) The kitchen, for stale and not spoiled: flat oxidized amber is fine in a braise or batter. What never happens: a pitcher, a comp, a michelada, the house pour. A free bad beer earns the same review a paid one does."],
  ] },
  { key:"styles", title:"The Styles", rows:[
    ["Bottle and can service",
     "Open it in front of the guest, always — a bottle opened out of sight is a trust problem. Wipe the top of a can before opening; it's been in a warehouse and on a truck. Offer a glass alongside unless they wave it off, and for anything craft, pour it for them. Never touch the rim of the glass or the mouth of the bottle. Check dates before you sell, not after a complaint. Leave the yeast sediment in the bottom of a bottle-conditioned beer, with one exception: a hefeweizen, where the traditional move is to rouse the last inch and pour it in — and where the polite move is to ask first."],
    ["Glass by style, briefly",
     "Shaker pint for American lagers and pale ales, because it's cheap and it stacks. Nonic 20 oz for English ales. Pilsner flute or pokal where clarity and the rising bead are the show. 0.5 L weizen vase for hefeweizen, because that enormous head is the point of the style. Tulip or teku for IPAs, saisons, and anything aromatic. Chalice for Belgians, usually etched at the base to keep a steady bead going. Snifter for barrel-aged and high-ABV pours of 8 to 10 oz. Stange for kölsch. The rule under the rules: the more aroma a beer has, the more the glass should curve inward to hold it."],
    ["Counting beer against a guest",
     "A US standard drink is 0.6 oz of ethanol — a 12 oz beer at 5%. A 16 oz pour of a 7% IPA is about 1.9 standard drinks. A 10 oz Belgian tripel at 9.5% is about 1.6. Guests count glasses; you count alcohol. The table on their fourth round of double IPAs is eight drinks deep and describing it as 'just beers,' and the guest who ordered the tripel because it looked golden and light has no idea what they're holding. Say the number lightly and early — 'heads up, that one's 9.5' — because it's an easier conversation at nine than at eleven. Be honest with yourself about what the count is and isn't: it measures what you poured, not how impaired someone is, which moves with body weight, food, time and things you can't see. The count tells you when to start paying attention. Your eyes tell you the rest."],
    ["Beer glass families",
     "The straight American shaker pint is the worst common beer glass, and it is everywhere because distributors give them away. A nonic pint carries a bulge below the rim so stacked glasses don't chip each other and a wet hand has something to grip. A willi becher tapers slightly inward at the lip, holding a head and concentrating aroma, and it is the best single choice if you stock only one. A tulip's flare supports the big foam of Belgians and strong ales. A pilsner glass is tall and narrow to display color and bead. A weizen glass is tall with a bulged top to hold the enormous wheat-beer head. A snifter is for anything over roughly 9% that you want a guest to sip rather than swallow. Rinse the glass with cold water before you pour — a wet wall pours a cleaner head."],
    ["Beer temperature, by style",
     "Your draught system runs 36-38F, and that number is about the line, not the guest: cold beer holds its CO2 in solution and pours without turning to foam on the way to the faucet. What should reach the mouth is warmer and depends on the beer. Lagers and pilsners drink well at 38-45F. Pale ales and IPAs want 45-50F. Stouts, barleywines and strong Belgians want 50-55F, which sounds alarming until you taste one at 38F and find it has no nose and no middle. Cold mutes aroma and bitterness, so the colder you serve, the less the guest gets of what they paid for — which is fine for a macro lager on a hot patio and a waste on a fourteen-dollar imperial stout. The glass does the last few degrees of work on its own; you just have to not fight it."],
    ["How style families actually work",
     "The first fork is not color, it is yeast. Ale yeast works warm, roughly 62 to 72F, and throws fruit; the clove and pepper side comes only from certain strains, so phenol is a fault on most taps and the style in a hefeweizen. Lager yeast works cold, roughly 45 to 55F, and that cold stops the esters forming; the rest that follows, near 32 to 38F for weeks to months, is maturation, where acetaldehyde and sulphur fade and the beer drops bright. A third family sits outside both, on wild yeast and bacteria: lambic, gueuze, Flanders red. Color settles nothing: a schwarzbier is near black and a lager, a kölsch is straw and an ale, a California common runs lager yeast warm on purpose. Past that it is four dials: how much malt and how dark it was kilned, how much hop and when it went in, which yeast and how warm it ran, and how strong it finishes. Hear those four and you can place a beer you have not tasted. Guidelines are a judging tool that follows brewers rather than binding them, though a few names are protected by law where they are brewed. Describe what is in the glass, and never argue with the label about strength, gluten or allergens."],
    ["Five questions that find a beer for anyone",
     "A guest who asks what is good is not asking your opinion, they are asking you to narrow the wall, and you can do it without using a single beer word. Ask first whether anything is off the table, because a guest avoiding alcohol or gluten is not stating a preference and no later question will surface it: non-alcoholic means under 0.5 percent alcohol by volume, not zero, and a beer labelled crafted to remove gluten starts as barley, cannot be verified gluten free, and is not an answer for a celiac guest. Then light or dark, knowing a twelve tap wall rarely holds more than two or three dark beers, so dark nearly finishes the job while light barely cuts, and that light often means not heavy rather than not dark, so ask which they mean. Bitter or not bitter, asked exactly that plainly, because hoppy means aroma to you and bitter to them. Strong or something they can drink all afternoon, which hands you the alcohol band. Familiar or new, the only one that asks about the guest. Half a minute puts you on three or four taps. Then name one and say why in a sentence, because three options hands the guest your job back."],
    ["Pairing: complement, contrast, cut",
     "Match weight before you match flavor, because a delicate beer under a rich plate disappears. After that there are three moves, they overlap more often than any list admits, and the discipline is naming the one you are leaning on. (1) Complement puts like beside like: a porter next to brisket off the smoker reads as one flavor, chocolate malt and burnt crust sitting in the same register. (2) Contrast sets opposite against opposite, sweetness against salt: a toffee-sweet brown ale beside cured ham, each side taking the edge off the other. (3) Cut is mechanical, not a flavor idea at all: bubbles and a dry finish, with bitterness or acid behind them, scrub fat off the tongue so the fourth bite tastes like the first, which is the pilsner beside fish and chips. The beers that cut hardest carry more gas than the 2.5 volumes most of your wall sits at, or they bring sourness instead, which is why a gose at 4% handles anything fried. Chile heat is settled on the Amber and IPA cards, so read those rather than reasoning it out at the rail. When you are unsure, cut. Fat and salt are on most plates you serve."],
    ["The flight, built properly",
     "There is no single flight spec. Four glasses at 4 oz and five at 3 oz are both common, so are 5 oz pours and six-glass trays, and some states limit what you may set in front of one guest at once, so check your own rules before printing a size. Sixteen ounces is a full pint of liquid, more than a headed pint actually delivers, so price it off your real pour cost rather than off the word pint. Count the alcohol too, not the glasses: four 4 oz pours off the strong end of your wall run nearer two standard drinks than one, while the guest reads a flight as tasting rather than drinking. Order it by intensity, not color, since a pale strong hazy flattens a dry stout behind it. Put sour and heavily fruited last because acid and fruit coat the palate and wreck what follows, not because they clear it. Mark the paddle and say the order once, flagging by position any glass a guest has to trust, since nonalcoholic beer under 0.5 percent still carries alcohol and a gluten reduced beer is not a gluten free one. Then name each pour and ask which they want full."],
    ["The taster pour is a sales tool",
     "The Sour and Gose card says to pour a taster first. Generalise it into policy with a number on it: a 1 to 2 oz taster, poured before the sale rather than after the complaint. Check your state rule first, because some states cap a free sample and a few forbid giving alcohol away. The arithmetic is not close. A sixtel holds 661 ounces, so off a 110 dollar keg a 2 oz taster costs about thirty cents, three-tenths of one percent of the keg, while the pint you comp is seven times the liquid, allowing for the head, plus the sale you never made. Offer it by naming the beer rather than asking whether they are sure, because that one is properly tart, try a sip takes three seconds and reads as handled instead of doubted. Pour it into a clean taster glass off the tap you are selling. Two limits keep it a tool and not a free-beer window: you offer it, they do not order it, and it is one per guest. It is still alcohol, so it counts, and it is never the pour you hand somebody you are slowing down. Anyone tasting the whole wall wants a flight, which has a price."],
    ["Selling up from the guest's own words",
     "A guest hands you a brand, not a style, so unpack it into attributes out loud. 'I like Guinness' means roast without hop bite, a light body, and a texture that comes from the nitro tap rather than from the beer, so match that texture on a nitro line or tell them why the porter feels different. Roast is the attribute that travels: porter and dark mild carry it, brown ale is the step away from it, and the guest on a 4.2 percent pour will not thank you for a 10 percent imperial that shares the word. 'I like IPA' splits into two camps, so ask one question: bitter and piney, or soft and juicy. Piney keeps them on West Coast, pale ale for less alcohol. Juicy sends them to hazy. Belgian IPA is not that fruit, it is yeast fruit and pepper, and it can run three points stronger, so say the number before you pour it. 'I do not like beer' often means 'I do not like cold macro lager,' a fair thing to dislike, so try hefeweizen, wit or the cider; but hear the rest of it, because it can also mean gluten, or not drinking. Offer three names at most and back one with a 2 oz taster."],
    ["Gluten, calories and carbs",
     "Two words, not interchangeable. Gluten-free beer is brewed with no barley, wheat or rye at all, from sorghum, millet, rice or buckwheat, and it tests under 20 parts per million, the federal limit. Gluten-reduced beer is ordinary barley beer treated with an enzyme that cuts the gluten into fragments the standard assay can no longer see, and those fragments can still make a celiac guest ill. So federal rules forbid the gluten-free claim there: the label must say processed, treated or crafted to remove gluten, and carry the line that its gluten content cannot be verified and it may contain gluten. Read them the label. 'That one is gluten-reduced, brewed from barley, and the label says it may still contain gluten.' Never say it is probably fine, and cider is only gluten-free until it pours through a barley beer's line. Calories are simpler, because ethanol carries 7 calories a gram and volume times ABV drives the count ahead of carbohydrate: roughly 150 in a 12 oz at 5 percent, near 100 with about 5 grams of carbohydrate in a 12 oz light lager at 4.2 percent, near 300 in a 16 oz hazy at 7 percent. Answer with the smallest glass of the lowest ABV, and a 20 oz nitro pour is not small."],
    ["Non-alcoholic beer is a real category",
     "Three ways a beer ends up without alcohol, and they do not taste alike. Limited fermentation stops the yeast early or uses a strain that cannot eat maltose, so the alcohol never arrives and the beer reads sweet and worty. Vacuum distillation drops the pressure until alcohol boils near 95F instead of 173F, which spares the beer a cooked flavor, but aroma is more volatile than alcohol and leaves with it, so it gets captured and dosed back or dry hopped in after. Reverse osmosis drives beer through a membrane, strips alcohol out of what passes, returns the water, and usually lands closest to the original. Then the line, and get this one exactly right. Non-alcoholic in the US means under 0.5 percent, not zero. Alcohol free is the stricter term; a 0.0 on the label is a brand's word, and some still test in traces. Tell any guest who says they need none, asked or not, and say nothing to anyone who did not. The other half of the answer is that 12 ounces at 0.5 is a tenth of a standard drink. Pour it like the rest of the wall, and date and rotate it, because alcohol was one of the hurdles holding spoilage off."],
    ["Beer in cocktails",
     "Beer goes in last and never into a sealed tin: the gas breaks out the moment you shake, blows the tin off the glass, and a tin that holds pours flat. Shake or stir the rest without it, strain into the serving glass, top down a bar spoon or the wall, and do not stir after. A radler is roughly half lager, half sparkling lemonade; a shandy is the British version, on lemon soda or ginger beer. Neither is soft: half of a five percent lager still runs near two and a half percent, five times the 0.5 percent ceiling for a non-alcoholic malt beverage, so never hand one to a guest who asked for none. Disclose the Worcestershire in a michelada, since it carries anchovy and usually barley malt vinegar, and a beer treated to remove gluten is not gluten-free. Layer by density: a dry stout near 1.010 rides on an English pale ale near 1.014, while most modern pale ales finish drier and will not hold it. Brut sparkling wine sits near 0.99, lighter than stout, so a black velvet takes the stout first. Ask before dropping a shot, and call the layered pint a half and half, because the other name is a paramilitary unit."],
  ] },
];

const ONTAP_REF = [
  { dom:"styles", name:"American Lager", cat:"Lager", note:"The highest-volume category in the country, and genuinely difficult to brew well because there is nowhere for a flaw to hide. The guest ordering it is buying cold and familiar, not complexity, which is a legitimate thing to want. Pour it with the same care you'd give the IPA.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.2-5.0%"], ["Glass","16 oz shaker pint"], ["Serve","38F on draught, colder from the bottle cooler"], ["Tastes like","Corn sweetness, faint cracker malt, almost no hop — clean and cold is the whole point"], ["Sells to","The guest who says 'just a beer.' Wings, burgers, fried and spicy food"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Pilsner", cat:"Lager", note:"The lager with hops: German pils runs drier and more bitter, Czech pils rounder and more golden. It is also the most fragile beer on your wall — pilsner shows staleness and dirty lines before anything else does, so when a pils tastes off, suspect your system before you blame the brewery.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.4-5.4%"], ["Glass","Pilsner flute or pokal"], ["Serve","38-45F"], ["Tastes like","Bready-cracker malt with a spicy noble-hop snap and a bone-dry finish"], ["Sells to","The lager drinker who wants flavor. Oysters, sausage, salty fried anything"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"American Light Lager", cat:"Lager", note:"Ordering light is a decision about pace, not a gap in the guest's palate, so the trade-up you were about to attempt reads as a correction. Do the arithmetic with them instead: at 4.2 percent a 12 oz bottle is 0.84 of a standard drink, so a table four deep has had three and a half, not four. Pour it cold, and pour it at the same speed as everything else.",
    facts:[["ABV","3.5-4.2%"], ["Glass","16 oz shaker pint"], ["Serve","38-42F, the coldest thing you pour"], ["Tastes like","Rice or corn thinness, a whisper of sweetness, carbonation doing most of the work"], ["Sells to","The long session and the guest pacing it. Wings, nachos, ballpark food, hot patios"], ["Calories","95 to 110 per 12 oz, against about 145 for a full lager"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Helles", cat:"Lager", note:"Munich's answer to Bohemian pilsner, and the answer was to turn the hops down and let the malt carry it. That is the pour for the guest who told you your pilsner was too sharp: same clean lager finish, none of the snap. Do not call it light, call it soft, and pour it in a half litre where the malt has room.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.7-5.4%"], ["Glass","0.5 L mug or willi becher"], ["Serve","42-45F"], ["Tastes like","Soft bready malt in front, hops only balancing, a clean short finish"], ["Sells to","Lager drinkers who find pilsner sharp. Pretzels, sausage, schnitzel, roast chicken"], ["Pour","a 0.5 L mug is 16.9 oz to the line and the foam rides above it, so it is a bigger serve than your pint. Price it accordingly"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Dunkel", cat:"Lager", note:"Munich drank this for centuries before pale kilning made helles possible, so sell it as the parent style rather than a seasonal novelty. Munich malt taken further gives bread crust and light toffee with no roast anywhere, and a shade more body than the helles beside it. It is the pour for a brown ale drinker who wants a lager finish.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.5-5.6%"], ["Glass","0.5 L mug or willi becher"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Bread crust and light toffee from Munich malt, a nutty middle, dry finish, no roast at all"], ["Sells to","Brown ale drinkers and the dark-curious. Roast pork, sausage, mushrooms, rye bread"], ["Color","dark copper to brown, and it passes light at the edge. If it reads black, you are holding the schwarzbier"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Marzen / Oktoberfest", cat:"Lager", note:"Two beers share this name. The Munich tents pour a pale golden festbier now, while the amber toastier marzen is the older recipe and the one most American breweries put on a seasonal label. Either way it lands near 6 percent against the 4.2 your light lager guest counted all summer, which is half a standard drink more in every glass.",
    facts:[["ABV","5.3-6.5%"], ["Glass","0.5 L mug, or a Mass at a fest"], ["Serve","42-48F"], ["Tastes like","Toasted bread and light caramel, fuller body, dry enough to finish the litre"], ["Sells to","The autumn crowd and the guest who wants more malt than a helles. Sausage, roast pork, pretzels, aged gouda"], ["Pour","a Mass is 33.8 oz, about 3.4 standard drinks at 6 percent"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Bock", cat:"Lager", note:"A bock is a lager, which nobody expects from something this dark and this strong. Cold fermentation and weeks of lagering are why 6.8 percent finishes clean rather than sticky. The goat on the label is a pun on the town the style came from, and the pun is the whole reason the animal is there. Pour it smaller than a pint.",
    facts:[["ABV","6.3-7.2%"], ["Glass","0.4 L pokal or willi becher"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Deep bread crust and toffee, rich and clean, warmth without heat, low bitterness"], ["Sells to","Winter drinkers stepping up from amber. Braises, smoked meat, stew, aged cheese"], ["Pour","12 to 13 oz, not a pint"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Doppelbock", cat:"Lager", note:"The fasting monks and their liquid bread is tradition more than documented history, so tell it as a story and not as a fact. What matters behind the bar is that 7 to 10 percent of smooth dark malt carries almost no bitterness to warn a palate. Most labels still end in ator, after the original. Eight ounces, and say the number.",
    facts:[["ABV","7.0-10.0%"], ["Glass","0.4 L pokal or snifter"], ["Serve","50-55F"], ["Tastes like","Dark bread, raisin, toffee and molasses, full body, warming, barely bitter"], ["Sells to","The after dinner table and port drinkers. Braised beef, venison, blue cheese, chocolate"], ["Pour","8 to 10 oz, never a pint"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Schwarzbier", cat:"Lager", note:"Black from a small charge of dehusked dark malt, which leaves the color behind and takes the burnt edge with it, so you get cocoa without the bite. It pours darker than the brown ale beside it and still drinks at lager weight through a whole shift. The one black beer on your wall you can sell by the half litre.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.4-5.4%"], ["Glass","0.5 L mug or willi becher"], ["Serve","42-48F"], ["Tastes like","Black in the glass and light on the tongue: dry cocoa, a clean coffee edge, no burn"], ["Sells to","Stout drinkers who want a session. Grilled meat, smoked sausage, pizza, burgers"], ["Demo","tilt a full glass to the light and the edge reads garnet, not black"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Vienna Lager", cat:"Lager", note:"Austrian in origin and kept in production by Mexican breweries, though the telling gets compressed behind the bar: the ambers in your cooler are cousins, sweeter and adjunct brewed, and at least one of them is labeled a dunkel. Toast without sweetness is the trick, so it does an amber ale's job for a guest who does not want an ale.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.7-5.5%"], ["Glass","Willi becher or 0.5 L mug"], ["Serve","42-48F"], ["Tastes like","Amber-copper, toasted malt without caramel sweetness, crisp dry finish, gentle hop"], ["Sells to","Amber ale drinkers and the taco table. Grilled meat, tacos, sharp cheddar, mole"], ["Lineage","Austrian brewers carried the malt and the method to Mexico in the late 1800s, and the style outlived its home"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Czech Dark Lager", cat:"Lager", note:"Rounder and sweeter than a German dunkel, finishing higher and darker: cola, plum and caramel where the dunkel gives bread crust. Both traditions decoct, so that is not the difference; the darker caramel malt and the sweeter finish are. Straight out of the box it is brown water, and a few degrees of warmth is the whole card.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.4-5.8%"], ["Glass","0.5 L mug or pokal"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Cola, dark bread and plum, rounder and sweeter than a dunkel, soft finish"], ["Sells to","The dessert table and the guest who orders sweet. Roast duck, pork, dumplings, gingerbread"], ["Cut pour","half dark and half pale in one glass is rezane, a common Czech order, and plenty of pubs will not pour it"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Rauchbier", cat:"Lager", note:"Beechwood fires in the kiln put the smoke in the malt, so it is deliberate and it does not fade with time in the keg. Half your guests flinch at the first sip and a good share come back to it by the third, which is the trade's story about this beer rather than anything measured. Never more than one on tap.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.8-6.0%"], ["Glass","0.5 L mug or willi becher"], ["Serve","42-48F"], ["Tastes like","Beechwood smoke over a marzen base: bacon and campfire, then a clean lager finish"], ["Sells to","Mezcal and peated whisky drinkers. Barbecue, bacon, smoked cheese, sausage, anything charred"], ["Taster","2 oz before you sell a full glass, every time"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Kellerbier / Zwickelbier", cat:"Lager", note:"Unfiltered and softly carbonated on purpose, so tell the floor before the handle goes up or it comes back over the bar for looking wrong. The yeast still suspended in it is what shortens the window, which is a different clock from a hazy and a gentler one. Order in sixtels, taste the tap midweek, and keep it out of a stein.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.7-6.0%"], ["Glass","0.5 L mug or willi becher, glass rather than stoneware so the haze shows"], ["Serve","42-48F"], ["Tastes like","Hazy helles or marzen, softer and rounder, bready yeast, lower carbonation"], ["Sells to","Helles drinkers and anyone who orders unfiltered wheat. Pretzels, sausage, fried food, soft cheese"], ["Shelf life","45 to 60 days cold, shorter than a filtered lager and longer than a hazy"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Hefeweizen", cat:"Wheat", note:"The banana and clove come from the yeast, not from any fruit — a thirty-second explanation that reliably sells the second round. It throws an enormous head, and that's the style working, not a bad pour. The lemon wedge is an American addition that most Bavarians consider vandalism, so ask rather than garnishing on autopilot.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.5-5.6%"], ["Glass","0.5 L weizen vase"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Banana and clove from the yeast, soft wheat body, essentially no bitterness"], ["Sells to","Guests who say they don't like beer. Brunch, salads, sausage, roast chicken"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Witbier", cat:"Wheat", note:"Genuinely spiced with coriander and dried orange peel, which is why the orange slice offends less here than elsewhere — though it's a Blue Moon marketing habit, not a Belgian tradition. Wit and hefe are both cloudy wheat beers and guests mix them up constantly: wit is spiced and tart, hefe is banana and clove.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.5-5.5%"], ["Glass","Tulip or weizen vase"], ["Serve","40-45F"], ["Tastes like","Coriander and dried orange peel over hazy wheat, tart, light, faintly creamy"], ["Sells to","White-wine drinkers. Mussels, goat cheese, salads, brunch"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Dunkelweizen & Weizenbock", cat:"Wheat", note:"One yeast, two malts: the banana and clove now ride on bread crust, raisin and a little cocoa instead of pale wheat, at exactly the strength of the hefeweizen beside it. Weizenbock is the trap on this card, a bock at 8% wearing that same soft wheat body, and it carries a wheat beer's carbonation, so it foams at bock strength and wants a glass with room.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.5-5.6% dunkel; 6.5-9.0% weizenbock"], ["Glass","0.5 L weizen vase; 0.3 L weizen glass or tulip for weizenbock"], ["Serve","45-50F; 50-55F for weizenbock"], ["Tastes like","Bread crust, raisin and a little cocoa under the wheat yeast's banana and clove; low bitterness, soft body, plum and warmth at bock strength"], ["Sells to","The hefeweizen regular who wants more of the same beer. Roast pork, sausage, mushrooms, apple and stone-fruit desserts"], ["Pour","10 to 12 oz on a weizenbock, which is what a 0.3 L weizen glass holds"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"American Wheat", cat:"Wheat", note:"Not a hefeweizen and not trying to be. American ale yeast is neutral, so you get wheat's soft body and cracker grain with none of the banana or clove, and that is the selling point rather than a shortfall. Plenty of them still pour hazy, so the cloud here promises nothing. It is the pour for the guest who wanted something lighter than a pale ale.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.0-5.5%"], ["Glass","16 oz shaker pint or willi becher"], ["Serve","40-45F"], ["Tastes like","Soft wheat body, cracker malt, faint lemon, brisk and clean: no banana, no clove"], ["Sells to","The hefeweizen drinker who didn't want the banana. Fish tacos, grilled shrimp, chicken, patio afternoons"], ["Garnish","ask first, but a lemon wedge is genuinely at home on this one"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Pale Ale", cat:"Ale", note:"The most useful beer on any list because it's the bridge. When a guest says they want 'something with flavor but not bitter,' this is the answer every time — and it's the pour that converts a lager drinker into a regular who trusts your recommendations.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.5-6.2%"], ["Glass","Shaker pint or tulip"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Caramel malt spine under citrus and pine hops, balanced, sessionable"], ["Sells to","The guest ready to leave lager. Burgers, pizza, sharp cheddar"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"IPA (West Coast)", cat:"Ale", note:"Bitterness and alcohol both sharpen the perception of capsaicin, so the IPA a guest orders with the hottest wings on the menu makes the wings worse; steer them to the amber or the wheat and they'll thank you. Remember the arithmetic too: a 16 oz pour of 7% IPA is about 1.9 standard drinks and it drinks like one.",
    facts:[["ABV","6.0-7.5%"], ["Glass","Tulip or nonic pint"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Resinous pine and grapefruit, firm bitterness, dry finish"], ["Sells to","Hop chasers. Rich, fatty, salty food — but not chile heat"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Hazy IPA", cat:"Ale", note:"The most perishable beer you sell. Fresh, it tastes like orange juice; six weeks later it goes onion, garlic and cardboard, and the guest blames you rather than the calendar. Order small and often, tape the pack date on the keg, and taste your own tap weekly.",
    facts:[["ABV","6.0-8.0%"], ["Glass","Tulip or teku"], ["Serve","42-48F"], ["Tastes like","Opaque and soft — mango, citrus pith, stone fruit — with bitterness dialled way down"], ["Sells to","People who bounced off West Coast IPA. Fried chicken, tacos, mango and chile"], ["Shelf life","30-45 days, the shortest on your wall"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Amber / Red Ale", cat:"Ale", note:"The safest recommendation on the wall and the one nobody ever asks for by name. Reach for it when a guest wants 'something in between,' and reach for it when they're eating something hot — residual malt sweetness blunts chile heat where hop bitterness sharpens it.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.5-6.0%"], ["Glass","Shaker or nonic pint"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Toasted caramel malt, restrained hops, a sweet-leaning finish"], ["Sells to","The undecided middle. BBQ, roast chicken, burgers, genuinely spicy food"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Blonde / Golden Ale", cat:"Ale", note:"An ale brewed to lager rules: pale, clean, and bitter only if you go looking for it. That is why the guest who came in for a domestic will take a second one, and why it settles a table where one person wants flavor and another wants familiar. When somebody asks for something local and means something safe, pour this.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.0-5.5%"], ["Glass","Shaker or nonic pint"], ["Serve","40-45F"], ["Tastes like","Soft bread and honey malt, a trace of citrus hop, clean and dry through the finish with no bitter edge at all"], ["Sells to","Lager drinkers and the table that cannot agree. Salads, fish tacos, wings, brunch"], ["Substitute for","the macro lager, when it blows"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Session IPA", cat:"Ale", note:"Brewed lean on purpose rather than watered down, which is why the fourth one still tastes like something. It is the hoppy beer to put in front of the table that started at noon, and the one place on your wall where a 16 oz pour needs no warning. Keep counting anyway: four of these is closer to five drinks than four.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.0-5.0%"], ["Glass","Willi becher or shaker pint"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Grapefruit and lemon hop aroma over a lean cracker body, bitter but brief, dry and light in the mouth"], ["Sells to","Hop drinkers on their third round. Long afternoons, lunch, fried food, fish tacos"], ["Standard drinks","16 oz at 4.5% is about 1.2"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Double IPA", cat:"Ale", note:"Pour it at 12 oz as house policy and print the price that way, or a guest reads a short pour as a slight. Two of them is most of a night for most people, and the one who wants a third will ask. When one comes back as too sweet, check the tap date before you argue the point.",
    facts:[["ABV","7.5-10%"], ["Glass","Tulip or teku"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Dense resin and candied citrus over a heavy malt middle, firm bitterness and clear alcohol warmth carrying the sweetness"], ["Sells to","The IPA drinker who wants more of everything. Blue cheese, short rib, carrot cake, rich salty food"], ["Pour","12 oz; 16 oz at 9% is about 2.4 standard drinks"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Black IPA", cat:"Ale", note:"It is the one dark beer on the wall you serve at IPA temperature and not stout temperature, and pouring it at 52F is what makes it taste heavy and muddled. The name does the damage, so lead with the hops and the finish, mention the color last, and let the guest see it after they have heard what it is.",
    facts:[["ABV","5.5-9.0%, usually 6.5-7.5%"], ["Glass","Tulip or nonic pint"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Pine and grapefruit hop over dehusked dark malt, so the color arrives without the roast bite; dry, and lighter in the mouth than it looks"], ["Sells to","The guest torn between the IPA and the porter. Grilled meat, smoked brisket, charred vegetables, aged cheddar"], ["Also sold as","Cascadian dark ale, American black ale"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Cold IPA", cat:"Ale", note:"The name is about the yeast and the cold conditioning, not the serving temperature, and the guest will ask. Its close cousin is the India pale lager, which is a lager with hops added; this is an IPA built on lager yeast, hopped harder and dried out further. Sell it to the pilsner drinker who is sure every IPA is sweet and heavy.",
    facts:[["ABV","6.0-7.5%"], ["Glass","Willi becher or tulip"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Bright citrus and pine hop with nothing sweet underneath, lager-clean through the middle, finishing short and dry"], ["Sells to","Pilsner drinkers who want hops. Fried chicken, sushi, sharp cheese, salty snacks"], ["Built from","lager yeast fermented warm, plus rice or corn for dryness"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Brown Ale", cat:"Dark", note:"Dark color, light body — the single most useful thing you can tell a guest who believes dark means heavy. Straight out of a 38F box it tastes like nothing at all, so let the glass stand a minute before you walk it over, or tell the guest to give it one.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.2-5.6%"], ["Glass","Nonic pint"], ["Serve","50-55F"], ["Tastes like","Hazelnut, toffee and light chocolate, low bitterness, dry finish"], ["Sells to","Whiskey and bourbon drinkers. Roast meat, mushrooms, aged cheese, nuts"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Porter", cat:"Dark", note:"The friendly dark beer: stout's flavor without the burnt-espresso edge, because porter leans on chocolate malt where stout uses roasted barley. When a guest wants a stout but finds them harsh, this is the pour that keeps them in the category.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.5-6.5%"], ["Glass","Nonic pint or tulip"], ["Serve","50-55F"], ["Tastes like","Chocolate and coffee, smooth through the finish, no sharp roast bite"], ["Sells to","Dessert, BBQ, chocolate, anything smoked"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Stout", cat:"Dark", note:"Dry Irish stout is a light beer wearing a dark coat — Guinness Draught is 4.2% and about 125 calories per 12 oz, under a Budweiser and over a light lager, which is worth saying out loud to the guest who calls it heavy. On nitro it's a two-part pour: three-quarters full, roughly ninety seconds to cascade, then top upright. Imperial stout is a different animal: 8 to 10 oz in a snifter, and say the ABV out loud.",
    facts:[["ABV","3.8-5.0% dry Irish; 8-12% imperial"], ["Glass","20 oz tulip pint on nitro; snifter for imperial"], ["Serve","42-45F on nitro; 50-55F for imperial"], ["Tastes like","Roasted barley, coffee bitterness, dry finish, dense creamy head on nitro"], ["Sells to","Oysters, shepherd's pie, steak, chocolate desserts"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Baltic Porter", cat:"Dark", note:"A porter recipe fermented cold, most often with lager yeast and months of lagering, which is why it carries 8 percent with no fusel heat. Guests read the word porter and expect the 5 percent pour from the dark section, so the number has to come out of your mouth before the glass leaves your hand.",
    facts:[["ABV","6.5-9.5%"], ["Glass","Snifter or 0.4 L pokal"], ["Serve","50-55F"], ["Tastes like","Dark fruit, licorice and molasses over a restrained roast, full bodied and smooth, no burnt edge"], ["Sells to","Imperial stout drinkers in cold months. Braises, smoked fish, blue cheese, dark chocolate"], ["Pour","10 oz in a snifter, never a pint"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Saison", cat:"Belgian & Sour", note:"The most food-flexible beer you sell and the easiest upsell to the table that ordered a bottle of wine last visit — 'dry, sparkling, peppery' lands with those guests every time. The bone-dry finish and heavy carbonation scrub fat off the palate the way a sparkling wine does.",
    facts:[["ABV","5.0-8.5%"], ["Glass","Tulip"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Peppery yeast, dry as chalk, high carbonation, lemon and hay"], ["Sells to","Wine drinkers, especially sparkling. Mussels, charcuterie, herbs, fried food, cheese"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Sour / Gose", cat:"Belgian & Sour", note:"The bridge between your beer list and your cocktail list — pitch it as a beer that drinks like a sour and pour a taster first, because half of guests love it instantly and half find it alarming. Warn whoever cleans lines: a sour or a heavily fruited beer leaves a stubborn print, and the pilsner that follows it down that line will taste wrong.",
    facts:[["ABV","3.0-5.0%"], ["Glass","Tulip or pilsner glass"], ["Serve","40-45F"], ["Tastes like","Bracing lemon tartness; a gose adds salt and coriander on top"], ["Sells to","Cocktail drinkers and the hot-afternoon crowd. Ceviche, fried food, goat cheese"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Belgian Strong (Dubbel, Tripel, Quad)", cat:"Belgian & Sour", note:"The dangerous one. A tripel is golden, effervescent and drinks like a 5% beer at 9.5%, so the guest who orders two has had a bit over three standard drinks without noticing. Serve it in a chalice at a smaller volume, say the number out loud when you set it down, and count it against them accordingly.",
    facts:[["ABV","6.5-12%"], ["Glass","Chalice or tulip"], ["Pour","8-12 oz, never a full pint"], ["Serve","45-55F"], ["Tastes like","Dubbel: raisin and caramel. Tripel: golden, spicy, deceptively light. Quad: fig, rum, warmth"], ["Sells to","Wine and spirits drinkers. Cheese boards, roast pork, rich stews"]] },

  { dom:"styles", name:"Kölsch", cat:"Hybrid", note:"Warm ale ferment for the fruit, then weeks cold to scrub the rest away, which is how a beer with a pear note still finishes like a lager. Nobody orders the word, so sell the sentence: a German ale that drinks like a pilsner. And the word is protected, so what is on your wall is almost certainly Kölsch-style unless it was brewed in Cologne.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.4-5.2%"], ["Glass","0.2 L stange"], ["Serve","38-45F"], ["Tastes like","Soft pale malt with a faint pear and apple note, delicate hop, clean dry finish that stops short of a pilsner's bite"], ["Sells to","The guest who finds pilsner too bitter, and the table that can't agree on one beer. Fried fish, schnitzel, cured ham, soft cheese"], ["Pour","0.2 L at a time, replaced often, so it never warms in the glass"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Altbier", cat:"Hybrid", note:"Copper does not mean sweet here. Alt carries as much bitterness as a pilsner and finishes dry, so the guest reaching for it as an amber wants a word first. Same hybrid trick as Kölsch, warm ale ferment for the malt depth then weeks cold near freezing, and those cold weeks are what make the bitterness smooth instead of rough.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.3-5.5%"], ["Glass","0.2 L altbier becher, or a willi becher"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Copper-bright, toasted Munich malt over a dry bready spine, firm noble bitterness that lingers, no caramel sweetness"], ["Sells to","Amber drinkers who want less sweetness. Pork, mustard, aged gouda, fries"], ["Sticke","the stronger seasonal version, around 6%, poured in smaller glasses"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"Cream Ale", cat:"Hybrid", note:"First question at the bar, and the answer is no: no cream, no lactose, no dairy anywhere in the style, and the name is about a smooth body. American brewers built it to answer the domestic lager, corn and all, often with lager yeast or a long cold rest after an ale ferment. Sell it to the one guest at a hazy table who wanted a cold yellow beer.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.2-5.6%"], ["Glass","16 oz shaker pint"], ["Serve","38-45F"], ["Tastes like","Sweet corn and cracker grain, a little more body and hop than a macro lager, a trace of fruit, smooth finish"], ["Sells to","The domestic drinker at a craft bar. Pizza, fried fish, corn, hot dogs, ballgames"], ["Adjunct","flaked maize is traditional here, not a cost-cutting shortcut"]] },
  { dom:"styles", name:"California Common", cat:"Hybrid", note:"The hybrid run backwards. Kölsch and alt are ale yeast finished cold; this is lager yeast worked warm, around 58 to 65F, which keeps the clean malt spine while letting the woody mint hop sit forward. Steam beer is a trademark, so most lists print California common, and that is the answer to the guest who asks what steam beer is.",
    facts:[["ABV","4.5-5.5%"], ["Glass","Shaker or nonic pint"], ["Serve","45-50F"], ["Tastes like","Toasty caramel malt cut by woody, minty hops, firm bitterness, a finish much drier than an amber's"], ["Sells to","The table split between an amber and a lager. Grilled steak, fries, onion rings, cheddar"], ["Hops","Northern Brewer, the woody mint note that defines the style"]] },
  /* The fault board. Filed under the faults section rather than styles,
     so a bartender with a bad pint in their hand lands on the whole board
     at once instead of hunting through fifty style cards. */
  { dom:"faults", name:"Diacetyl (buttered popcorn)", cat:"Fermentation", note:"Leftover precursor keeps converting in your cooler, so a keg that poured clean on Tuesday can turn buttery by Friday with nobody at fault but the fermentation. A line does the opposite of that; it gets worse keg after keg and never better. Published thresholds run about 20 to 100 ppb, so a pale lager confesses while a stout still hides it.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Movie popcorn butter, butterscotch, and a slick coating left on the roof of the mouth"], ["Comes from","Yeast pushes alpha-acetolactate out of the cell, that precursor turns into diacetyl in the beer on its own, and the yeast then has to take the diacetyl back up and reduce it away. Chill or rack the beer off before it finishes that job and the butter stays. Or Lactobacillus and Pediococcus living in a dirty line, making it straight off the sugars"], ["Correct in","A trace in Czech pale lager, and a low butterscotch under the malt in cask English bitter and ESB. Never the headline even there, and a plain fault in anything pale, hoppy or American"], ["Threshold","About 100 ppb, a tenth of a ppm, and trained palates catch it near 20 ppb in a pale lager, higher wherever roast or hops cover it"], ["Your move","Taste the same beer out of a can if you stock it, and ask whether the last keg on that faucet did this too. Clean in the package, or two kegs running buttery on one line, and it is the line. 86 the tap and pull it for a caustic clean"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Acetaldehyde (green apple)", cat:"Fermentation", note:"Guests call this sour and you'll be tempted to agree. It's not acid. It's unfinished beer, oxidized beer, or a dirty line, and the first two point opposite ways: a green keg won't clean up on your gas, an oxidized one gets worse every day. That threshold is high, so anything you can smell is the whole keg, not a bad pour.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Bruised green apple, cut pumpkin, fresh emulsion paint, a cidery edge on the finish"], ["Comes from","Yeast makes it on the road to ethanol and takes it back up during conditioning, so beer pulled off the yeast early arrives still carrying it. Oxygen remakes it from ethanol, and the acetic bacteria in a dirty line run the same step on their way to vinegar."], ["Correct in","Low green apple is accepted in the big pale lager families, American lager and the international pale lagers most Mexican imports sit in, but accepted is not aimed at. Everywhere else it's a fault, and on those lagers too once it's the first thing you smell instead of a trace under everything."], ["Threshold","Commonly cited at 10 to 25 ppm (mg/L); most finished beer carries a few ppm and stays under it."], ["Your move","Smell every other tap before you touch anything. All of them means your lines, not the beer. One tap alone means that keg, so put a different keg on that line to be sure, then 86 it and leave the beer in the keg, because nothing you do behind the bar takes it back out."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Sulphur: match, egg and cooked corn", cat:"Fermentation", note:"Three compounds wear one name, so test before you comp. Swirl hard and smell again after a minute: match and egg purge with the CO2 and that pint is fine to sell. Cooked corn will not leave. Drain and rubber usually are not the beer at all, so pour the second one into a glass off a different rack.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Struck match and spent fireworks is sulphur dioxide; boiled egg, drain and sewer is hydrogen sulphide; cooked sweetcorn or tinned vegetable is DMS"], ["Comes from","Yeast building its own sulphur amino acids. It reduces sulphate to make methionine and cysteine and vents the excess as hydrogen sulphide and sulphur dioxide, and the CO2 of a healthy fermentation scrubs most of that back out, so what lingers is beer whose fermentation was cut short or whose yeast ran stressed or short of nitrogen. DMS is not a fermentation compound at all: it comes from malt as S-methylmethionine, converted in the boil and carried off with the steam, so a covered or hurried boil leaves it in the beer. In your cellar, egg can also be autolysed yeast in an unfiltered keg that sat warm, or sulphate-reducing bacteria in a line nobody cleaned."], ["Correct in","A faint match or eggy note in young pale lager is normal and it blows off, and lager yeast throws more of it than ale yeast does. A whisper of cooked corn is to style in pale lager, American and Czech especially, and nowhere else. The flinty match note of a Burton pale ale is genuine, but it is the water, several hundred ppm of sulphate working through fermentation, not something the brewer dosed in at the end. In all three the level is the whole argument: the moment a guest can name it as egg or drain, it has stopped being style."], ["Threshold","Three compounds, three different numbers, and the unit is where this bites you. Hydrogen sulphide is about 5 ppb, sometimes quoted to 10, low enough that a trace shows. Sulphur dioxide is about 10 to 25 ppm, thousands of times higher, so a struck-match note you can actually smell is a far bigger dose than an eggy one you can smell. DMS is around 30 ppb, and a pale lager often sits right about there on purpose."], ["Your move","Swirl the glass hard and smell again after a minute. If match and egg go, the beer was young and the pint is fine to sell. If it holds, or if it reads cooked corn, nothing is leaving and that one is a dump. Then work out whether it is the beer or the building: smell a clean dry glass off a different rack, smell the drain and the mat under it, and pour the same beer a second time. Every tap showing it is a line or cellar problem. One tap only points at the keg."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Autolysis (yeast digesting itself)", cat:"Fermentation", note:"Your cold box is not where this usually happens. It starts at the brewery, in beer left warm and long on the yeast bed, and reaches you already made. In your building it shows up in unfiltered kegs and in bottle-conditioned beer stored warm for months. Not every yeasty beer is this one. Marmite is literally autolysed brewer's yeast, which is why the smell reads as food.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Soy sauce, Marmite, meat stock, a rubbery sulfur edge, sometimes a goaty soapy slick on the finish, and a savoury heaviness that won't rinse off the palate"], ["Comes from","Yeast that has run out of food digesting itself from the inside: its own enzymes break the cell down and spill amino acids, nucleotides, fatty acids and sulfur compounds into the beer. Time and warmth on the yeast do it, usually at the brewery before the keg ever ships."], ["Correct in","None in beer, at any level. The bready, doughy yeast note of an unfiltered or bottle-conditioned beer is to style and is not this; the lees character built on purpose in traditional-method sparkling wine is the same self-digestion run slow and cold on healthy yeast."], ["Threshold","No published figure for autolysis itself, because it is a mixture and not one compound. The fatty acids it frees do have beer thresholds, in parts per million: octanoic acid near 15, hexanoic near 8, decanoic near 10. Those markers read goaty and soapy, so they do not measure the soy sauce side at all."], ["Your move","Prove it is the keg and not the line, then read the pack date and tap date, pull the tap, and leave the beer in the keg for the claim. Nothing downstream touches it; the compounds are dissolved in the beer, so cleaning, gas and a fresh glass all pour you the same pint."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Estery, banana and pear drop", cat:"Fermentation", note:"Half the estery beers sent back at your bar are correct, and a hefeweizen without banana is the broken one. Learn the split. Isoamyl acetate is the banana, and it belongs in wheat beer at several times threshold. Ethyl acetate is the nail varnish, and every beer you pour holds some; it only reads as solvent past about 30 ppm. Neither one grows in a cold keg.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Ripe banana and pear drop sweets, a candy lift over the malt; the nail varnish note behind it is a second compound, not more banana"], ["Comes from","Yeast builds both during fermentation at the brewery, joining acetyl-CoA to an alcohol: isoamyl alcohol gives you the banana, ethanol gives you the solvent. Strain decides most of it, then warm ferment, underpitching, thin wort aeration and high gravity push it higher. The profile is fixed when the keg is sealed"], ["Correct in","Bavarian wheat above all, so hefeweizen, dunkelweizen and weizenbock, where banana runs two to four times threshold and is the point of the beer. A lighter pear and orange lift is to style in tripel and Belgian golden strong. Witbier is coriander and orange, saison is pepper and citrus, so full banana in either is a fault"], ["Threshold","Isoamyl acetate about 1.2 ppm, and a hefeweizen sits near 3 to 4 ppm. Ethyl acetate about 30 ppm, but clean beer already carries 10 to 30 of it, so that is a line you cross, not a compound that appears"], ["Your move","Name the style before you call it a fault. In a helles, pour another tap first; one estery line that poured clean last week is live wild yeast working in the keg, and it will be gaining carbonation and drying out too. 86 that tap and log it for whoever handles the claim"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Fusel and hot alcohol", cat:"Fermentation", note:"Heat is not proof of strength. A clean 11 percent quad drinks smooth and a badly fermented 5 percent pale can scorch, so read the tap list before you judge the glass. The tempting belief is that it will blow off or mellow in the keg. It will not, because the yeast finished making it weeks before the keg reached you.",
    facts:[["Reads as","A spirituous rasp on the nose, a burn down the throat and chest, heat out of proportion to the ABV; sharp nail polish is ethyl acetate, not this"], ["Comes from","Higher alcohols the yeast builds from wort amino acids while it grows, pushed up by a warm ferment, a high gravity wort, over oxygenation, too small a pitch, or a strain chosen for it"], ["Correct in","Nowhere as heat. Barleywine, imperial stout, tripel and quad all want a round ethanol warmth and name hot or solventy alcohol a fault; below threshold these same alcohols are wanted, isoamyl alcohol being the precursor of the banana ester in hefeweizen and tripel"], ["Threshold","Isoamyl alcohol 50 to 70 ppm, the largest single fusel; total higher alcohols run 60 to 120 ppm in ordinary beer and 150 to 250 in strong ale, turning harsh above about 300"], ["Your move","Check the ABV first, then decide: a round warmth in a 10 percent beer is the style, a rasp in a 5 percent one is a faulted keg, and nothing in your lines, gas or cooler causes fusel heat or cures it"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Phenolic, clove and pepper", cat:"Fermentation", note:"Clove and plastic are the same chemical family and opposite verdicts, which is why the word phenolic gets thrown at a correct hefeweizen. The one you should fear is chlorophenol, detectable near 200 parts per trillion, a thousand times under the clove figure, so a trace of bleach on one glass or in one line is enough. That fault is almost always something your bar did.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Clove and white pepper at best, plastic, Band-Aid or mouthwash at worst"], ["Comes from","POF-positive yeast decarboxylating the malt's ferulic acid into 4-vinyl guaiacol, or chlorine meeting the beer's own phenols on a glass, in a line or in the water"], ["Correct in","Hefeweizen above all, roughly 2 to 4 ppm against a 0.3 ppm threshold; witbier, saison and Belgian ales sit far lower, spice rather than the lead. Rauchbier smoke is guaiacol off the kilned malt, a different compound and not the yeast's work"], ["Threshold","4-vinyl guaiacol near 0.3 ppm, which is 300 ppb; chlorophenol near 200 to 300 parts per trillion, or 0.2 to 0.3 ppb"], ["Your move","Split clove from plastic against the style first. Plastic in a clean beer, smell a dry glass off the rack and the rinse water before you blame the keg, because chlorine rides the glass and the line"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"DMS, cooked corn", cat:"Process & Water", note:"Check your own lines before you call the brewery: bugs in a dirty line reduce DMSO to this same compound, so taste the taps either side first. Faint sweet corn on a Czech pils or a helles is usually just the malt and not a fault at all. Rotten cabbage or shellfish is spoilage, and that one you pull and leave sealed.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Creamed corn, canned corn, tomato juice; cooked cabbage or shellfish as it climbs, and louder in a warm glass"], ["Comes from","Pilsner malt SMM converted by heat and never boiled off: short or covered boil, long hot stand, slow cooling. The rotten version comes from elsewhere, bacteria reducing DMSO in the keg or in your lines"], ["Correct in","Czech pale lager and German helles or pils, at or near threshold, where pilsner malt makes it: a faint sweet corn is on style. The corn in American light lager is mostly the maize adjunct, not this"], ["Threshold","30 to 60 ppb (0.03 to 0.06 ppm), and plenty of pale lagers sit right on it"], ["Your move","Style card first, then a can of the same beer, then the taps either side: the can shows it too and it's the brewery, several taps and it's your lines, one tap alone and it's that keg"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Astringency", cat:"Process & Water", note:"You'll call it too bitter and blame the hops. Bitterness is a taste that peaks and fades; astringency is a feel that builds and stays on the gums after you swallow. There is no ppm to chase, because a mouthfeel has no threshold. The cause you own is a short rinse after caustic, a burn risk before it is a taste one, so 86 the tap.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Chalky drying grip on the gums and teeth, like stewed tea or grape skin; it builds sip over sip and outlasts the swallow"], ["Comes from","Husk and hop polyphenols that bind and strip the proteins lubricating your mouth, which is why it stacks up rather than fading; extraction climbs with runnings pH past about 6 and with alkaline water, and heat matters less than the old sparge rule claims. At the bar: caustic left in after a short rinse, or detergent film on the glass"], ["Correct in","Low and in the finish only: dry and roasty stouts carry a coffee-like grip, and oak-aged sours and Flanders reds carry barrel tannin. Heavy dry hopping rasps in a very fresh hazy and settles with a week or two cold, so that one is a flaw but not a claim. Chalky and building on the gums is a fault in every style"], ["Threshold","No detection threshold; it is a feel, not a compound. Sound beer carries roughly 150 to 350 ppm total polyphenol and drinks clean. Training kits spike tannic acid in the tens of ppm, which is a dosing level, not a published threshold"], ["Your move","Retaste from a fresh rack first, since a filmed glass fakes it. If one tap dries, pull it and flush that line with water until it runs clean; if every tap dries, the rinse after caustic was short on all of them. Serve nothing off it until a pH strip on the run-off reads neutral"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Metallic", cat:"Process & Water", note:"Your instinct is to blame the brewery, and metallic is the one fault most likely to be your own hardware: a rusted coupler spring, a faucet worn through the chrome onto brass, a steel washer somebody cheaped out on. You will not find it by sniffing the glass, since it shows up on the swallow. And a pitted part gets replaced, not scrubbed.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Wet penny, blood, ink; a tinny suck at the back of the tongue that arrives on the swallow more than in the glass"], ["Comes from","Ferrous metal or bare plating in the beer path: a rusted coupler spring, a steel washer or clamp, a faucet worn through the chrome so beer sits on brass and pulls copper and zinc. Iron in the water does it too, and that is iron, not hardness. Trace metal also drives staling, so it often rides in with cardboard."], ["Correct in","None, in any style. The flinty sulfate bite of a Burton pale ale gets called metallic and isn't, and neither is the dry grip of tannin."], ["Threshold","Iron, about 0.1 ppm; 0.05 on a sharp palate and 0.3 unmistakable. Parts per million, not billion"], ["Your move","Taste a can of the same beer as your control, since the liner keeps beer off metal. Every tap metallic points at water or the cleaning rinse; one tap points at hardware, so pull that coupler and faucet and look for rust and bare brass."]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Chlorophenol, plastic and band-aid", cat:"Process & Water", note:"You will call this infection and blame the keg, and the cold rinse before a pour is not what did it. A bleached bar towel, a chlorine sanitizer final rinse, or bleach anywhere near a line is. Chlorophenol soaks into vinyl and gaskets, so a tainted line gets replaced, not cleaned, and trace parts per trillion still taste.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Band-aid, antiseptic, electrical tape, melted plastic; it coats the mouth and is still there long after you swallow"], ["Comes from","Chlorine or chloramine chlorinating the phenols malt puts in the beer: bleach on a surface, a chlorine sanitizer final rinse, chloraminated water. Chloramine is the stubborn form because it does not gas off or sit out. Mould and yeast then methylate chlorophenols into chloroanisoles, which read the same and soak into vinyl and rubber"], ["Correct in","Never, in any beer. The band-aid note that is to style is Brett's 4-ethylphenol in lambic, gueuze and Flanders red, and that one reads barnyard and leather rather than antiseptic. Hefeweizen clove and rauchbier smoke are different phenols again"], ["Threshold","Parts per trillion, and it depends which compound: about 10 ppt for the sharpest chlorophenols, up to a few ppb for the weakest, chloroanisoles lower again. Chloraminated water runs 0.5 to 4 ppm, around a hundred thousand times the low figure, and only a trace has to convert"], ["Your move","Fresh glass off a different rack first: if that one pours clean, it was the glassware and not the tap. Every tap tainted is your lines or your water; one tap is that line or that keg, so 86 it and leave the keg coupled and full for the rep. A bleached vinyl line and its gaskets get replaced, not rinsed"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Acetic (vinegar)", cat:"Microbial", note:"You hear tart and think infected, but a sour beer's acidity is mostly lactic, and lactic is smooth and clean. Acetic is the volatile edge on top, so it reaches your nose before your tongue, and that is how you tell them apart. It is nearly always oxygen in your building rather than the brewery's fault. Some is in every beer, so the call is how much.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Vinegar in the nose before it reaches your tongue, then a nail-polish lift; it catches in the throat and finishes thin and hard, where a lactic sour stays smooth"], ["Comes from","Acetobacter turning ethanol into acetic acid, which it cannot do without oxygen, so it lives where beer meets air: a faucet reassembled damp, biofilm in a line past its caustic date, a keg on a pump. The nail polish is ethyl acetate, which yeast makes in every beer and which that acid adds to by esterifying with ethanol. Heterofermentative Lactobacillus in the same biofilm makes acetic acid with no air at all"], ["Correct in","Flanders red, which is the one style built more acetic than its neighbours and still never meant to read vinegar-like, and less of it in oud bruin, which is maltier and lactic-led. Not lambic or gueuze: that acidity is meant to be lactic, and a vinegary or cidery note there is called a fault by the brewers themselves. A kettle sour or gose should show none"], ["Threshold","Acetic acid about 130 ppm, published figures spanning roughly 70 to 200, against 55 to 145 ppm in ordinary beer, so you are calling a line crossed rather than a compound present; ethyl acetate about 30 ppm, and normal fermentation already puts beer at 12 to 25"], ["Your move","Repour from a different rack, then taste another beer off a different line: one tap is that keg or that line, every tap is your building. 86 the tap, pull and soak the faucet and run caustic; if a fresh keg still reads vinegar on the clean line, the rest stays in the keg for the claim"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Lactic (sour)", cat:"Microbial", note:"The wrong belief is that souring needs air. It does not: these are anaerobes that work fine in a sealed line, which is why the fault shows up first on your slowest tap, the seasonal nobody orders. Vinegar is the one that needs oxygen. And butter alongside the tartness does not name the bug on its own, though a slick, ropy pour with it points at Pediococcus.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Tart palate over a faint nose, yoghurt and sourdough, none of the vinegar sting you smell across the bar; the hops go dull under the acid"], ["Comes from","Lactobacillus and Pediococcus fermenting sugars to lactic acid without oxygen, so a sealed line, faucet or coupler grows them; spoilage strains tolerate hops, and some make acetic acid as well"], ["Correct in","Berliner weisse, gose, lambic and most American sours, where it runs to thousands of ppm at pH 3.2 to 3.5; Flanders belongs too, but there acetic sits beside the lactic and is to style"], ["Threshold","Lactic acid near 400 ppm, the published beer figure, and a taste threshold since the acid barely smells; buffering moves it, and clean beer sits at pH 4.0 to 4.6"], ["Your move","If the style is not a sour, 86 that tap and tag the line, leave the rest in the keg for the claim, and sell the can instead"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Butyric (rancid butter)", cat:"Microbial", note:"Do not let the word butter fool you into calling this diacetyl. Diacetyl reads sweet and slick, like popcorn, and it coats the tongue; butyric reads sour and spoiled, nearer sick than butter, and warming the glass only sharpens it. It is not farmhouse funk either, so it never leaves your hand with an apology attached. Trust the flinch.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Rancid butter going to baby sick and old parmesan; it clings to the empty glass"], ["Comes from","Not Clostridium in your beer, which is too acid and too alcoholic for it. Clostridium makes butyrate in sweet wort at the brewery, so that keg arrives faulted. Your half is biofilm: beer standing diluted and weak in a coupler, FOB chamber or dead leg, the one pocket where a bug like Megasphaera can still work"], ["Correct in","Nothing you can smell. A trace sits under threshold in plenty of beer, and lambic makes some in its first weeks, but years in wood turn most of it into ethyl butyrate, the pineapple note. A blender counts a butyric nose as a fault too"], ["Threshold","About 2.2 ppm in beer, the number the trade quotes, with panels landing between 2 and 5. That is milligrams per litre, a thousandfold coarser than the parts per billion faults, so if you can smell it there is plenty of it"], ["Your move","Dump the pour, never the keg. Pull a check glass off two other taps: all of them means your lines, so 86 and clean tonight. One tap alone means leave the beer in the keg, photograph the date code and call the rep while the claim is still provable"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Mousy", cat:"Microbial", note:"These compounds are weak bases, so at beer's pH they sit protonated and barely volatile, which is why the glass smells clean; near your saliva's neutral pH they come free, and that is both the delay and the long finish. Sensitivity varies wildly and some people read nothing at all, so one clean palate proves nothing. The numbers come from wine work, in water not beer.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Usually nothing on the nose, then mouse cage, stale cracker and corn chips arriving a few seconds after the swallow, and hanging on long after the beer is gone"], ["Comes from","Brettanomyces and lactic acid bacteria building N-heterocycles, mainly 2-acetyltetrahydropyridine (ATHP), its ethyl form and 2-acetylpyrroline, out of the amino acids lysine and ornithine plus ethanol. Oxygen drives the reaction, so a slack keg, a weeping seal, a stale growler or anything pushed with air is what feeds it"], ["Correct in","None. Even in a lambic or a mixed-fermentation beer sold on its Brett character this is a fault, not the funk you were promised"], ["Threshold","Parts per billion, and the spread is wide: in water ATHP is cited near 1.6, 2-acetylpyrroline near 0.1 and the ethyl form near 150. A real beer matrix raises all three, so hold these as an order of magnitude, not a line"], ["Your move","Swallow and wait ten seconds before you call anything, then rub a drop on the back of your hand, let it dry and smell it. If it reads, 86 the tap"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Oxidation", cat:"Age & Package", note:"Beer carries a little sulfite of its own that binds those aldehydes and hides them, so an oxidized keg does not slide, it falls off a cliff: honest on Tuesday, papery on Friday. Your palate drifts along with it, which is why you taste a slow line weekly against a fresh can of the same beer instead of trusting memory.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Wet cardboard and stale bread; sherry or toffee in dark beer, hops gone dull"], ["Comes from","Oxygen taken on at fill, turned into radicals by trace iron and copper; much of the cardboard aldehyde forms in the brewhouse and is only released later in the package, faster warm"], ["Correct in","Barleywine, old ale, barrel-aged stout, Flanders red, after years in glass or wood: sherry and dried fruit, never paper, and never a keg you left warm"], ["Threshold","trans-2-nonenal about 0.1 ppb, that is 0.1 microgram per litre (0.03 to 0.1 cited)"], ["Your move","Check the other taps for the same note first, comp the guest, 86 the tap, leave the beer in the keg, photograph the collar, call the rep"]] },
  { dom:"faults", name:"Light-struck", cat:"Age & Package", note:"No trick fixes this one, so do not chill it harder or pour it into a fresh glass and hope. The damage happened in the package and it never fades. Do not blame your lines either, because a dirty line has its own set of faults and skunk is not among them. A pint that reads skunky and never saw light is some other sulfur.",
    facts:[["Reads as","Skunk spray, sharp and rubbery; it reaches you before the glass lands and a swirl will not blow it off"], ["Comes from","Riboflavin absorbs light at roughly 350 to 500 nm and hands the energy to hop iso-alpha-acids, breaking off a side chain that takes up sulfur already in the beer to form MBT, 3-methyl-2-butene-1-thiol"], ["Correct in","None, in any style. Where a familiar import always tastes that way, that is light it took on the way to you, not the recipe, and the same brand kept dark does not show it"], ["Threshold","MBT about 4 ng/L, roughly 4 parts per trillion, not per billion; published figures run about 1 to 10 ng/L"], ["Your move","Replace it, then pull the rest of that case because it took the same light, move that stock out of the window and off the display shelf, and keep the patio ice bucket covered"]] },
];

/* The same KNOWLEDGE bank the quiz already draws from. All nineteen carry
   topic:"ontap", which the quiz split minted for them before this move. */
KNOWLEDGE.push(
  /* --- beer --- */
  { q:"A faucet that has sat untouched for twenty minutes pours a glass of pure foam, and the next glass pours clean. Most likely cause?",
    options:["The keg is nearly empty", "The CO2 regulator has drifted high and unbalanced the line", "Beer in the warm faucet broke out of solution", "The glass came off the washer still warm"],
    a:2, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"The faucet is the warmest point in a draught system, and warm beer releases its CO2. A mis-set regulator, a blowing keg and a hot glass all foam too, but all three would foam the second pour as well — the fact that it self-corrects is the whole diagnosis." },
  { q:"The standard cooler and line temperature for a direct-draw draught system is…",
    options:["38F", "34F", "30F", "45F"],
    a:0, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"38F holds a typical 2.4 to 2.7 volumes of CO2 in solution at normal applied pressure. Colder buys you nothing and risks it: the coldest corner of a box set to 34F can dip under 28F, where beer actually freezes, and above 40F the gas breaks out inside the line and every pour foams." },
  { q:"Most American domestic and craft kegs use which coupler?",
    options:["S system", "U system", "A system", "D system"],
    a:3, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"D (American Sankey) is the US default; S covers most European imports, U the Irish stouts, A and M certain German brands. The probe geometry actually differs, so a coupler that won't seat with a quarter turn is the wrong coupler, not a stuck one." },
  { q:"A half-barrel keg holds 15.5 gallons. Roughly how many 16 oz pours is that before waste?",
    options:["165", "124", "141", "198"],
    a:1, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"15.5 gallons is 1,984 fluid ounces, or 124 sixteen-ounce pours. 165 is what the same keg yields in 12 oz servings, and 141 is the tempting wrong turn — that's the count if you divide by the 14 oz of liquid actually in a headed pint rather than by the size of the glass." },
  { q:"You wet the inside of a glass and the water breaks into beads instead of running off in a sheet. That means…",
    options:["The glass is beer-clean — water beads up on a properly washed glass", "The glass is colder than the beer", "Your water supply is unusually hard", "There's a fatty or detergent film on the glass"],
    a:3, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"Water sheets evenly off clean glass and beads on film. That film — grease, lipstick, or a fat-based sanitizer residue — will flatten the head on every beer you pour into it, and the guest will blame the beer." },
  { q:"A full pint has tiny bubbles clinging in patches to the inside of the glass wall. What does that tell you?",
    options:["The beer is over-carbonated", "The applied pressure is set too high", "The glass has a film on it", "The beer is perfectly conditioned"],
    a:2, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"Bubbles need a nucleation site, and on a beer-clean glass the CO2 rises in a steady bead from the base rather than sticking to the wall. Cling means film, and film means the head will collapse and the glass will come back without lacing." },
  { q:"The 'beer gas' behind a nitro stout tap is…",
    options:["Roughly 50/50 nitrogen to CO2 at 12 to 14 psi", "Roughly 75/25 nitrogen to CO2 at 30 to 40 psi", "100% nitrogen at 30 to 40 psi", "Roughly 25/75 nitrogen to CO2 at 30 to 40 psi"],
    a:1, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"Nitrogen barely dissolves in beer, so it can be pushed hard without over-carbonating the keg, while the small CO2 fraction holds the beer's own carbonation in solution. Flip that ratio and 30-plus psi of mostly-CO2 turns the keg into a foam bomb; drop to pure nitrogen and the beer slowly goes flat." },
  { q:"The correct nitro stout pour is…",
    options:["Fill about three-quarters, let it settle, then top it upright", "Fill three-quarters and top it straight away, settling in front of the guest", "Fill to the rim in one pull and let the overflow carry the foam off", "Fill halfway, wait for the cascade, then finish it at 45 degrees"],
    a:0, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"The cascade has to finish before you can build the dome, which is why the two-part pour takes about two minutes and why topping it early just collapses back on you. Handing over a still-cascading glass looks fast and drinks wrong." },
  { q:"The industry standard cleaning cadence for draught lines is…",
    options:["Caustic weekly, acid monthly", "Caustic monthly, acid yearly", "Caustic every two weeks, acid quarterly", "Caustic quarterly, acid only as needed"],
    a:2, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"Two-week caustic kills the biofilm; quarterly acid removes beerstone, which is calcium oxalate and which caustic will not touch. A bar that runs caustic faithfully and skips the acid step still ends up pouring buttery beer, and never understands why." },
  { q:"Every pour from one tap has a good head but a butterscotch or vinegar edge. The keg was tapped four days ago, and the same beer out of a can tastes fine. First suspect?",
    options:["The keg itself is past its pack date", "A slow CO2 leak has flattened the keg", "Lipstick or detergent residue on the glassware", "Bacteria and wild yeast growing in the beer line"],
    a:3, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"The can rules out the brewery and four days rules out age; an off flavor isolated to one tap lives in that tap's line, where biofilm produces diacetyl and acetic notes within days. Stale beer goes papery, not buttery, and a dirty glass kills head retention without adding flavor at all." },
  { q:"The correct glass for a hefeweizen is…",
    options:["A 0.2 L stange", "A 0.5 L weizen vase", "A 10 oz snifter", "A 16 oz shaker pint"],
    a:1, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"A hefe throws an enormous head and the tall curved vase is built to hold it, because that head is where the banana and clove aroma sits. The stange belongs to kölsch and the snifter to high-ABV dark beer." },
  { q:"A 16 oz pour of a 7% IPA counts as roughly how many US standard drinks?",
    options:["About 1.9", "About 1.3", "About 1.0", "About 2.8"],
    a:0, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"A US standard drink is 0.6 oz of ethanol, which is a 12 oz beer at 5%; sixteen ounces at 7% is 1.12 oz of ethanol. 1.3 is what you'd get if that pint were a 5% lager, and the gap between those two numbers is the whole reason you count alcohol instead of glasses." },
  { q:"SCENARIO — A guest two sips into a pint says the beer tastes flat. The pour looked normal. Your first move is…",
    options:["Check the CO2 tank and the applied pressure before anything else", "Explain that the style is naturally low in carbonation", "Pour a fresh one into a glass from a different rack and compare", "Comp it and pour them something else entirely"],
    a:2, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"Checking the gas is genuinely defensible, since a drifting regulator really does flatten a keg over a couple of days — but a film on one rack of glasses kills the head on perfectly carbonated beer, and the glass test takes fifteen seconds standing right there. If the fresh glass also falls flat, then go look at the gas." },
  { q:"SCENARIO — A keg blows mid-rush with six tickets on the rail. Best sequence?",
    options:["Finish the other five tickets first, then swap the keg quietly", "Swap it immediately and serve the first foamy pour with an apology", "86 the beer for the night and offer the table a substitute", "Tell the guest a time, swap it, dump the first pour, then serve"],
    a:3, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"Finishing the other tickets first is defensible when a barback can swap the keg for you, but if you're alone the guest whose beer stopped mid-pour is the one watching you work, and silence reads as being forgotten. Give them a number before you disappear, and never hand over the foam pint you already knew was coming." },
  { q:"SCENARIO — A keg lands off a warm truck at 4pm and you need it on tap by 6. Best move?",
    options:["Get it cold, let it rest two hours, then tap and pour one off", "Tap it now so the line has time to settle before service", "Roll it on its chime a few times to settle the beer, then tap", "Tap it now and pour off a pitcher of foam right away"],
    a:0, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"Warm agitated beer is foam, and tapping a warm keg early doesn't cool it — two hours cold is the bare minimum and overnight is the real answer. Rolling it agitates the beer further, which is the opposite of settling." },
  { q:"Guinness and Harp kegs in the US use which coupler?",
    options:["D system", "U system", "S system", "A system"],
    a:1, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"U covers the Irish stouts and ales, while D handles most American kegs, S most European imports, and A and M certain German brands. Brand lists shift as importers change, so label your odd couplers and read the keg — forcing the wrong probe geometry breaks the valve." },
  { q:"Green and clear beer bottles are a problem mainly because…",
    options:["The glass is thinner and chips more easily at the rim", "Beer oxidises faster in lighter glass regardless of light", "Clear and green bottles cannot be pasteurized after filling", "Light passes through and skunks the beer within minutes"],
    a:3, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"Brown glass blocks most of the light that reacts with hop compounds to produce the skunk thiol; a clear bottle in a sunny window can turn in under a minute. Cans have no light-strike problem at all, which is a large part of why craft beer moved to them." },
  { q:"SCENARIO — At 9pm every tap on the wall starts foaming at once. Where do you look first?",
    options:["The faucets, which probably all need new seals", "The primary regulator feeding the whole gas manifold", "The walk-in temperature and whether the door is shut", "Each keg's coupler, checking one line at a time"],
    a:2, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"Both the cooler and the primary regulator are shared causes, so both are worth suspecting — but the cooler is free to check, a door propped open during a restock is far more common than a regulator that jumps, and over-pressure needs days to over-carbonate a keg while a warm box foams within the hour. Rule out the door, then go read the primary." },
  { q:"Your draught faucets run about 38F. What temperature should actually reach the guest?",
    options:["38F across the board, in a glass out of the freezer", "Lagers 38-45F, ales 45-50F, stouts and strong ales 50-55F", "Everything at 55F, so the aromatics have room to open", "Everything at 45F, a fair compromise across every style"],
    a:1, topic:"ontap",
    explain:"Lines run cold because cold beer holds its CO2 and pours without foaming, not because 38F is the right drinking temperature for a stout. Cold mutes aroma and bitterness, so the beers you charge the most for are the ones that should warm a few degrees in the glass." },
);
